Guide.content._register("w19", {"id":"w19","order":19,"region":"finale","title":"Boss Rounds","short":"Boss rounds","tagline":"The interviewer moves the dials. Defend your answer anyway.","story":"The question has crossed every stop on the journey, and the interviewer starts changing the constraints halfway through your answer. Each boss round mixes chapters: a retrieval design that must survive an access review, an assistant that must be fast, cheap and available at once, and an agent platform where tools, peers and workflows share one set of rules. You are scored on clarifying first, choosing for stated dials, naming what flips the choice, and saying how you would know it works.","oneLiner":"Six mixed rounds where every answer crosses chapters, so you practise choosing, flipping and defending under changing constraints.","youWillLearn":["Carry one design across chapters: ingest, chunk, retrieve, guard and authorise a RAG answer, and say where each choice constrains the next","Trade latency, cost and availability against each other and against correctness and tenant isolation","Place functions, MCP servers, A2A peers, workflows and handoffs by ownership boundary, task shape and risk","Answer an open interview prompt with the five Dial-and-Defend steps inside a time box","Say out loud what flips your choice, how you would detect failure and which metric you would watch","Pull in the operating chapters (identity, evaluation, observability, governance) when a design has to ship","Revise with cross-chapter rapid-fire contrasts in the flashcard deck"],"minutes":150,"prereqs":["w00","w01","w02","w03","w04","w05","w06","w07","w08","w09","w10","w11","w12","w13","w14","w15","w16","w17","w18"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w19-fc-001","front":"Pre-filter, post-filter, live check: what does each do to the top k?","back":"Pre-filter applies the caller's access rule inside the search, so top k holds only readable chunks. Post-filter fetches first, then drops, and starves k. Live check asks the source at query time: freshest, slowest.","tags":["rag","security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-002","front":"Part number versus paraphrase: which retrieval leg wins each?","back":"Keyword (BM25) wins exact identifiers, dates and names. Dense vectors win paraphrase and meaning. Hybrid runs both in parallel and fuses the ranks with RRF. Query rewrites can drop identifiers, so test them.","tags":["rag"]},{"id":"w19-fc-003","front":"RRF versus a weighted score mix: when do you pick which?","back":"RRF fuses ranks, so it needs no score calibration and works out of the box. A convex combination of normalised scores can beat it but needs labelled examples to tune the weight.","tags":["rag"]},{"id":"w19-fc-004","front":"What can a reranker not do?","back":"It reorders a shortlist and cannot find a document the first stage missed. The Azure semantic ranker reranks only the top 50 of the BM25 or RRF results, so fix recall before ranking.","tags":["rag"]},{"id":"w19-fc-005","front":"Parent-child versus contextual retrieval: how does each repair a chunk that lost its context?","back":"Parent-child matches small children and returns the larger parent. Contextual retrieval prepends LLM-written context to each chunk before embedding and BM25. Anthropic measured 35%, 49% and 67% fewer top-20 failures, the last with reranking.","tags":["rag","chunking"]},{"id":"w19-fc-006","front":"Why is re-embedding done blue-green and not in place?","back":"Vectors from different embedding models are not comparable, and Azure embedding models cannot be upgraded in place. Build a second index, backfill, compare on a golden set, repoint an alias, keep the old index for rollback.","tags":["ingestion"]},{"id":"w19-fc-007","front":"Prompt Shields, spotlighting, groundedness detection: what does each catch?","back":"Prompt Shields classifies user and document attacks (GA). Spotlighting marks untrusted text so the model discounts instructions in it. Groundedness detection (preview) checks answer claims against sources. Each is probabilistic.","tags":["guardrails"]},{"id":"w19-fc-008","front":"Security filters versus native ACL scopes in Azure AI Search, as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Security filters are GA: you resolve groups and store ACL strings on each chunk. Native ACL and RBAC scopes, Purview labels and SharePoint ACLs are preview: the user's token goes in the `x-ms-query-source-authorization` header.","tags":["security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-009","front":"What does a token carry when the user belongs to 300 groups?","back":"An overage indicator instead of the group list, because a JWT lists at most 200 groups. Call Graph `transitiveMemberOf` (999 per page at most) and fail closed on any error.","tags":["security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-010","front":"What does a citation prove, and what does it not?","back":"It proves provenance: which chunk a claim came from. It does not prove the claim is supported or true. Pair citations with a groundedness check, a score cutoff and abstention.","tags":["rag","guardrails"]},{"id":"w19-fc-011","front":"Slow first word or slow finish: which lever for each?","back":"First token is queueing plus prefill: shorten or cache the prompt. Total time is mostly output tokens: cap, trim and stream. OpenAI says halving output may halve latency, halving the prompt about 1% to 5%.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w19-fc-012","front":"Prompt cache versus semantic cache: what comes back and what is the risk?","back":"A prompt cache reuses KV state for an identical prefix, still runs the model and never changes the answer. A semantic cache returns a stored answer for a similar prompt, which can be wrong or belong to another user.","tags":["caching"]},{"id":"w19-fc-013","front":"Prompt cache prices on GPT-5.6 and later?","back":"Cache write 1.25x and read 0.1x of the uncached input rate (0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol). One write plus nine reads costs 2.15x against 10x uncached. The minimum prefix is 1,024 visible tokens.","tags":["caching","cost"]},{"id":"w19-fc-014","front":"`cached_tokens` is 0 on a long prompt. First suspect?","back":"The first 1,024 tokens differ between requests: a timestamp, user id or reordered tools near the top. Put stable content first, volatile content last, and keep tool lists in a fixed order.","tags":["caching"]},{"id":"w19-fc-015","front":"PTU, Priority, Batch, Flex: one line each.","back":"PTU reserves throughput and bills it whether used or not. Priority is a faster tier on standard quota. Batch is 50% off with a 24-hour target. Flex (preview) is 50% off with no SLA.","tags":["capacity","cost"]},{"id":"w19-fc-016","front":"Why can an Azure OpenAI 429 appear below your real usage?","back":"The TPM check counts prompt plus `max_tokens` (plus `best_of`) up front, so an oversized `max_tokens` eats quota. Capacity throttling and a temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard are other causes.","tags":["quota"]},{"id":"w19-fc-017","front":"Retry, hedge, circuit breaker: which problem does each solve?","back":"Retry fixes a transient error: backoff, jitter, a cap. A hedge sends a second copy of a slow call to cut tail latency and needs idempotency. A breaker stops calling a failing dependency so it can recover.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w19-fc-018","front":"Spillover versus regional failover on Azure?","back":"Spillover sends non-200 overflow from a PTU deployment to a standard deployment in the same resource. Foundry has no automatic regional failover: you build, size and test the second region yourself.","tags":["resilience","capacity"]},{"id":"w19-fc-019","front":"Router versus cascade: who decides and what does each cost?","back":"A router picks one model before the call and can misroute. A cascade tries a cheap model and escalates when a check fails, so hard queries pay twice. Azure model router Balanced stays within about 1% to 2% of top quality.","tags":["cost","routing"]},{"id":"w19-fc-020","front":"Chat Completions versus Responses: who holds the state?","back":"Chat Completions is stateless: you resend the messages. Responses can store state server-side (30 days by default) and chain with `previous_response_id`, which bills the earlier input tokens again.","tags":["api"]},{"id":"w19-fc-021","front":"Tools, resources, prompts: who controls each?","back":"Tools are model-controlled, resources are application-controlled and prompts are user-controlled. Put side effects in tools, attachable context in resources and slash-command templates in prompts.","tags":["mcp"]},{"id":"w19-fc-022","front":"MCP, A2A, agent-as-tool: which crosses which boundary?","back":"MCP joins an agent to tools and data. A2A joins independent, opaque agents across an ownership boundary using tasks. Agent-as-tool is a plain in-process call inside one codebase. Real systems combine them.","tags":["mcp","a2a"]},{"id":"w19-fc-023","front":"Why is token passthrough forbidden in MCP?","back":"A server must not accept or forward a token that was not issued for it. Exchange the inbound token for a downstream token with the right audience (on-behalf-of), or the server becomes a confused deputy.","tags":["mcp","security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-024","front":"What did MCP revision 2026-07-28 change about connections?","back":"It removed protocol sessions: no `initialize` handshake and no `Mcp-Session-Id` header. Every request carries protocol version and client capabilities in `_meta`, and servers must implement `server/discover`.","tags":["mcp"]},{"id":"w19-fc-025","front":"Handoff versus supervisor: who owns the user conversation?","back":"In a handoff the receiving agent takes ownership and no central orchestrator exists, so cap the turns to stop ping-pong loops. A supervisor keeps control and calls specialists as tools, so one agent owns the conversation.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w19-fc-026","front":"Agent, Harness Agent, Workflow: what does each add?","back":"An agent is one model loop with tools. A Harness Agent is the same agent with todos, plan and execute modes, standing approvals and history persistence on by default. A workflow is a graph where your code fixes the order.","tags":["agent-framework"]},{"id":"w19-fc-027","front":"Checkpoint, session, background response: what survives a restart?","back":"A workflow checkpoint, written per superstep, holds executor state, pending messages and requests, and shared state. A session holds conversation state. A background response is a provider-held run you poll with a continuation token.","tags":["agent-framework"]},{"id":"w19-fc-028","front":"When do you add a second agent?","back":"Start with one. Add agents when a named dial forces it: breadth beyond one context, tool overload or isolation. Anthropic measured about 15x chat tokens for multi-agent. Cognition warns that parallel writers make conflicting decisions.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w19-fc-029","front":"An A2A task is COMPLETED and the user wants a change. What is the next call?","back":"Terminal tasks cannot restart. Send a new message with the same `contextId`, optionally with `referenceTaskIds`, and the server creates a new task. Task ids are always server-generated.","tags":["a2a"]},{"id":"w19-fc-030","front":"What must hold across a tool-approval round trip in Agent Framework?","back":"The same `AgentSession`. By default an approval response authorizes a call only when it matches a pending request in that session. Disabling that binding can let a fabricated approval through. [[r51]]","tags":["agent-framework","security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-031","front":"What agent types does Foundry Agent Service offer, and which protocols do hosted agents expose?","back":"Prompt agents, voice-based prompt agents and hosted agents. Hosted agents expose Responses, Invocations, Invocations WebSocket, Activity and A2A, and one agent can expose several. [[r52]] [[r53]]","tags":["foundry"]},{"id":"w19-fc-032","front":"Who holds the credentials: an Entra agent identity or its blueprint?","back":"The blueprint. An agent identity is a service principal (`ServiceIdentity`) with no credentials of its own. In OBO the user is the token subject and the agent is the actor. In S2S the agent is the subject. [[r55]]","tags":["identity"]},{"id":"w19-fc-033","front":"When did Agent 365 reach GA, and for whom?","back":"2026-05-01, for the Commercial segment, licensed per user. It observes, governs and secures agents through a registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center, Microsoft Entra, Purview and Defender. [[r54]]","tags":["governance"]},{"id":"w19-fc-034","front":"What are Foundry's three evaluation stages?","back":"Base model selection, pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring. Offline golden sets gate releases, and online monitoring catches drift that the golden set never held. [[r56]]","tags":["evaluation"]},{"id":"w19-fc-035","front":"Why run the AI Red Teaming Agent more than once?","back":"Its Attack Success Rate (successful attacks over total attacks) is scored by a generative model, so results can be non-deterministic and include false positives. Review samples and track the trend. Preview. [[r57]]","tags":["evaluation","security"]},{"id":"w19-fc-036","front":"Which spans does Agent Framework emit, and is content captured by default?","back":"`invoke_agent <name>`, `chat <model>` and `execute_tool <function>`. Instrumentation should not capture prompts and outputs by default: they are opt-in attributes. The GenAI conventions carry Development status. [[r58]]","tags":["observability"]},{"id":"w19-fc-037","front":"Which EU AI Act dates matter for GenAI teams?","back":"In force 2024-08-01. Prohibitions and AI literacy from 2025-02-02. GPAI obligations from 2025-08-02. Systems placed on the market before 2026-08-02 have until 2026-12-02 for Article 50(2) marking under the AI Omnibus. [[r59]]","tags":["governance"]},{"id":"w19-fc-038","front":"NIST AI 600-1 in one line?","back":"The Generative AI Profile of the NIST AI RMF, released 2024-07-26. It lists twelve GenAI risks, with prompt injection and data poisoning under information security. [[r60]]","tags":["governance"]}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Indexer overview in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-indexer-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Managed pull ingestion: data sources, schedules, skillsets and which sources are GA or preview. Chapter w03.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Change and delete detection using indexers for Azure Storage","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-how-to-index-azure-blob-changed-deleted","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Why a blob indexer misses deletes and renames unless you plan for them. Chapter w03.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Chunk large documents for RAG and vector search in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-how-to-chunk-documents","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Chunking options, sizes and overlap, with the Text Split skill. Chapter w04.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/contextual-retrieval","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Chunk-level context plus BM25 plus reranking, with measured failure-rate cuts and a cost estimate. Chapter w04.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"Vector query filters in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-filters","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"preFilter, postFilter and strictPostFilter, and what each does to recall. Chapter w04.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Hybrid search overview in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"When keyword, vector and hybrid win, including product codes and names. Chapter w05.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Relevance scoring in hybrid search using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-ranking","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How RRF merges ranked lists and when it runs. Chapter w05.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r8","title":"Semantic ranking in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-search-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The reranker's input window, top-50 limit and score range. Chapter w05.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"Agentic retrieval in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"LLM query planning across knowledge sources, reasoning efforts and API versions. Preview features are labelled. Chapter w05.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r10","title":"Prompt Shields","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"User prompt and document attack detection and its API shape. Chapter w06.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"Groundedness detection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/groundedness","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Checks answer claims against grounding sources. Preview as of 2026-10-13. Chapter w06.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r12","title":"Guardrails and controls overview in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The four intervention points and which controls are preview for agents. Chapter w06.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r13","title":"Document-level access control in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-document-level-access-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The four approaches: security filters, native ACL and RBAC scopes, Purview labels, SharePoint ACLs. Mostly preview. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r14","title":"Query-time ACL and RBAC enforcement in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-query-access-control-rbac-enforcement","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The user token header, group resolution through Graph and elevated read. Preview. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r15","title":"Security filters for trimming results in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-security-trimming-for-azure-search","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The GA pre-filter pattern with `search.in`. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"The risk list that RAG and agent threat models map to. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"Microsoft identity platform and OAuth 2.0 On-Behalf-Of flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-oauth2-on-behalf-of-flow","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How a middle tier calls a downstream API as the user. Chapters w07 and w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Configure group claims and app roles in tokens","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/zero-trust/develop/configure-tokens-group-claims-app-roles","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Group claim limits and the overage indicator. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"Prompt caching (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"What makes a hit, write and read pricing, TTL and cache keys. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r20","title":"Prompt caching (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The Azure rules before and after GPT-5.6, including the 1,024-token prefix. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Prompt caching (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Automatic and explicit breakpoints, TTLs and multipliers. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r22","title":"Auditing Prompt Caching in Language Model APIs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07776","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (ICML 2025)","note":"Timing attacks that reveal shared prompt caches across users. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"llm-semantic-cache-lookup policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-semantic-cache-lookup-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"score-threshold semantics and partitioning with vary-by. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"Latency optimization (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Where latency comes from and which levers move it. Chapter w02.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r25","title":"Conversation state (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/conversation-state","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Stored responses, previous_response_id and Conversations. Chapter w01.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r26","title":"Migrate to the Responses API","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/migrate-to-responses","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"What changes from Chat Completions and why. Chapter w01.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Reasoning models (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Reasoning effort, hidden tokens and the output reserve. Chapter w01.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"What is provisioned throughput for Foundry Models?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"PTU billing, capacity and what quota does not guarantee. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r29","title":"Determine PTU sizing for a workload","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/provisioned-throughput-sizing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The sizing formula and spillover to a standard deployment. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r30","title":"Manage Azure OpenAI quota in Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"TPM and RPM allocation and the causes of a 429. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Why you get a 429 below your quota (Azure OpenAI, Dev Proxy)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-cloud/dev/dev-proxy/concepts/azure-openai-429-token-rate-limit","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How the up-front token estimate and the short rate windows trigger a 429. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r32","title":"Rate limits (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/rate-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Limit types, headers and the retry advice. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r33","title":"AI gateway capabilities in Azure API Management","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/genai-gateway-capabilities","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token limits, token metrics, semantic caching, safety and load balancing at the gateway. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r34","title":"High availability and resiliency for Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/how-to/high-availability-resiliency","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Recovery tiers, the lack of automatic failover and the approximate RTO of each. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r35","title":"Exponential Backoff And Jitter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/","type":"blog","publisher":"AWS Architecture Blog","note":"Why backoff without jitter still produces synchronized retry waves. Chapter w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r36","title":"Batch API (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"50% discount, 24-hour window and file limits. Chapters w01 and w16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r37","title":"MCP specification changelog, 2026-07-28","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"The stateless revision: no sessions, no initialize, server/discover. Chapter w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r38","title":"MCP server tools, 2026-07-28","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Tool definitions, annotations, errors and the ordering rule for tools/list. Chapter w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r39","title":"MCP authorization, 2026-07-28","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/authorization","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"OAuth 2.1, protected resource metadata, resource indicators and the passthrough ban. Chapter w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r40","title":"MCP security best practices","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices","type":"doc","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Confused deputy, token passthrough, SSRF and session risks with mitigations. Chapter w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r41","title":"Tool search tool (Anthropic)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/tool-search-tool","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"On-demand tool loading and the token cost of large tool catalogs. Chapter w08.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r42","title":"A2A Protocol specification","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/","type":"spec","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Agent Card, Task states, Message, Part, Artifact and bindings. Chapter w09.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r43","title":"A2A and MCP: detailed comparison","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/a2a-and-mcp/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project (Linux Foundation)","note":"Why the two protocols are complementary. Chapter w09.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r44","title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Workflows versus agents and the five workflow patterns. Dated 2024-12-19. Chapter w10.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r45","title":"How we built our multi-agent research system","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Orchestrator-workers in production, with token costs and failure lessons. Dated 2025-06-13. Chapter w10.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r46","title":"Don't Build Multi-Agents","url":"https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Cognition","note":"The counter-argument: parallel agents carry conflicting implicit decisions. Dated 2025-06-12. Chapter w10.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r47","title":"AI agent orchestration patterns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/ai-agent-design-patterns","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Three levels of complexity and the orchestration patterns. Chapters w10 and w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r48","title":"Microsoft Agent Framework overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/overview/","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What the framework contains and when not to use an agent. Chapter w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r49","title":"Agent Harness (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/concepts/harness","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What the Harness Agent adds to a plain agent and its defaults. Chapter w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r50","title":"Workflow checkpoints and resuming (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/workflows/checkpoints","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What a checkpoint holds, storage options and resume calls. Chapter w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r51","title":"Tool approval (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/tools/tool-approval","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Approval requests, standing approvals and session binding. Chapter w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r52","title":"Agents in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The agent types and components of Foundry Agent Service. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r53","title":"What are hosted agents?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/hosted-agents","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Protocols, sessions, sandboxes and versions for hosted agents. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r54","title":"Overview of Microsoft Agent 365","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Observe, govern and secure agents through a registry. GA for Commercial on 2026-05-01. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r55","title":"Agent identities in Microsoft Entra Agent ID","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/agent-id/agent-identities","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Blueprints, agent identities, sponsors and execution modes. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r56","title":"Observability in generative AI (Foundry)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The three-stage evaluation lifecycle and monitoring. Chapter w14.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r57","title":"AI Red Teaming Agent (Foundry)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/ai-red-teaming-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Automated adversarial scans and Attack Success Rate. Preview as of 2026-10-13. Chapter w14.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r58","title":"OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions (overview)","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/README.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"Span names, attributes, metrics and content capture rules, all in Development status. Chapter w15.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r59","title":"Timeline for the implementation of the EU AI Act","url":"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act","type":"doc","publisher":"European Commission AI Act Service Desk","note":"Dated obligations from 2024-08-01 onward. Chapter w17.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r60","title":"NIST AI 600-1: Generative AI Profile","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","type":"spec","publisher":"NIST","note":"Twelve GenAI risks and suggested actions, released 2024-07-26. Chapter w17.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r61","title":"Images and vision (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/images-vision","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Patch arithmetic, the multiplier and the detail budgets for image inputs. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r62","title":"Voice latency and cost (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/voice-latency-cost","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Realtime audio tokens, resent history and retention_ratio. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r63","title":"Voice activity detection (OpenAI Realtime)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/realtime-vad","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"server_vad against semantic_vad turn detection. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r64","title":"Use the GPT Realtime API for speech and audio (Azure)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/realtime-audio","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"WebRTC and WebSocket latency for Azure realtime audio. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r65","title":"Choose the right Foundry Tool for document processing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/choosing-right-ai-tool","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Containers, custom models and when to pick each document tool. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r66","title":"Content Understanding video overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/video/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How the video analyzer samples frames. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r67","title":"ColPali: Efficient Document Retrieval with Vision Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01449","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv, ICLR 2025","note":"Page-image retrieval and its storage cost against text embeddings. Chapter w13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r68","title":"Model router for Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-router","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Supported deployment types and context window behaviour of the router. Chapter w01.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r69","title":"Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The vLLM paper and its throughput gain over earlier serving systems. Chapter w18.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r70","title":"What is Foundry Local?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry-local/what-is-foundry-local","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"A single-user on-device runtime, not a server fleet. Chapter w18.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r71","title":"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15425","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Token count gaps between languages for the same text. Chapter w18.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r72","title":"Agents Rule of Two: A Practical Approach to AI Agent Security","url":"https://ai.meta.com/blog/practical-ai-agent-security/","note":"Never combine untrusted input, sensitive access and external action in one session without a human. Chapter w07.","type":"blog","publisher":"Meta","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r73","title":"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Zheng et al.)","note":"Judge biases including position bias and swap consistency. Chapter w14.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r74","title":"tau-bench: Tool-Agent-User Interaction in Real-World Domains","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Yao et al., Sierra)","note":"Defines pass^k for agent reliability across repeated trials. Chapter w14.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r75","title":"Trace agents client-side in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/trace-agent-client-side","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What the trace decorator records and how the content flag behaves. Chapter w15.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r76","title":"Agent Framework observability","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Telemetry setup and MCP trace context propagation. Chapter w15.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r77","title":"Content streaming in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/content-streaming","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Default and asynchronous content filter modes for streamed output. Chapter w06.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r78","title":"Incident response for AI systems","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/zero-trust/sfi/incident-response-ai-systems","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Response timelines and fixing the problem at its source. Chapter w17.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r79","title":"Agent identity concepts in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/agent-identity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token audience and RBAC for agents that call MCP servers. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r80","title":"Foundry portal general availability overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/general-availability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Which Foundry agent features are GA and which are not. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r81","title":"Migrate hosted agents to the latest version","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/migrate-hosted-agent-preview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retirement dates for earlier hosted agent previews. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r82","title":"Manage agent registry in the Microsoft 365 admin center","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/admin/manage/agent-registry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What the Agent 365 registry lists and who administers it. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r83","title":"Durable Extension (Azure Functions and your own compute)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/hosting/azure-functions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Human-in-the-loop pauses without compute and the package maturity. Chapter w11.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r84","title":"Resilience for long-running Microsoft Foundry hosted agents (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/long-running-agent-resilience","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Handler re-entry, leases and stream replay for hosted agents. Chapter w12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r85","title":"List a user's transitive memberships (Microsoft Graph)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/user-list-transitivememberof","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Page size and limits when expanding nested group membership. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r86","title":"Set up notifications for changes in resource data (Microsoft Graph)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/change-notifications-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Change notification delay for drive items. Chapter w07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r87","title":"NoLiMa: Long-Context Evaluation Beyond Literal Matching","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05167","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Accuracy of long-context models falls at 32K when the question shares no literal words with the answer. Chapter w18.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Dial-and-Defend","def":"The five-step answer method: clarify the dials, list the options, pick and say what flips the pick, name the failure modes, name the metrics and rollout."},{"term":"Boss round","def":"A mixed practice round that crosses chapters and ends in an open interview prompt with unspecified dials."},{"term":"Pre-filter","def":"An access or metadata filter applied inside the search so the top k holds only permitted chunks. Azure AI Search offers `preFilter`, `postFilter` and `strictPostFilter` (preview) for vector queries [[r5]]."},{"term":"Post-filter","def":"Dropping results after retrieval. Cheap to build, but it starves the top k under selective access and lets denied text reach rerankers, caches and logs."},{"term":"Live check","def":"A permission lookup against the source system at query time on the shortlist: the freshest answer at the cost of latency and source load."},{"term":"Reciprocal rank fusion (RRF)","def":"Merges ranked lists by summing a reciprocal of each document's rank per list, so it needs no score calibration [[r7]]."},{"term":"Abstention","def":"Declining to answer when retrieval is empty or weak, instead of letting the model improvise."},{"term":"Prompt cache","def":"Provider-side reuse of the KV state of a repeated prefix. It changes cost and time to first token, not the answer [[r19]]."},{"term":"Semantic cache","def":"A cache that returns a stored answer when a new prompt embeds close enough to an earlier one. It needs partitioning by user or permission scope [[r23]]."},{"term":"Spillover","def":"Sending overflow requests from a provisioned deployment to a standard deployment in the same resource [[r29]]."},{"term":"Circuit breaker","def":"Stops calls to a failing dependency for a cool-down period so it can recover and callers fail fast."},{"term":"Hedged request","def":"A duplicate of a slow request sent to another replica to cut tail latency. It needs idempotent calls and costs extra requests."},{"term":"Superstep","def":"One round of a graph workflow in Agent Framework: messages move between executors, and a checkpoint is written at the end [[r50]]."},{"term":"Opaque agent","def":"An agent whose prompts, tools and internal state stay hidden from its caller. A2A peers interact only through tasks and messages [[r42]]."},{"term":"Token passthrough","def":"Forwarding a token that was not issued for your server to a downstream service. MCP forbids it [[r39]]."}],"levels":[{"id":"w19-l1","order":1,"title":"Boss: RAG end to end, from ingest to a cited answer under access control","kind":"boss","minutes":30,"brief":"An interviewer hands you a mixed document estate and asks for an assistant that answers with citations and respects each person's access, with every dial unspecified. Carry one design from ingest to cited answer and defend what each stage promises the next one.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The interview prompt","level":2,"id":"the-interview-prompt"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["Employees ask questions over files, wiki pages and tickets that live in several systems","Every answer must carry citations","Each person may read only what the source systems allow","Two weeks after launch, part-number questions return wrong answers and security asks you to prove that a revoked contractor sees nothing"],"ask":["Which sources, how big is the corpus, and how fast does each source change?","What does a permission look like: tenant tag, groups, per-document rules, deny entries, sharing links?","How fast must revocation take effect for each sensitivity tier?","What share of the corpus does a typical caller see, and how skewed are tenants?","What is the time to first token target, and is preview acceptable in production?","Which questions are exact lookups (identifiers, dates, names) and which are paraphrases?"],"tension":"Freshness, recall, instant revocation and latency pull in different directions. Say which one you trade, and for which tier of content."},{"t":"h","text":"The path, stage by stage","level":2,"id":"the-path-stage-by-stage"},{"t":"flow","title":"One answer, five chapters","steps":[{"title":"Ingest","tag":"w03","md":"Detect change by etag or content hash, parse with layout analysis, redact PII and capture the ACL in the same pass. Make deletes visible with a tombstone and make every write idempotent."},{"title":"Chunk and embed","tag":"w04","md":"Split along structure with a parent id and keep tables whole. Copy the ACL fields onto every chunk. Re-embed through a second index and an alias, never in place."},{"title":"Retrieve","tag":"w05","md":"Hybrid search with the access filter inside the query, rerank the top 50, then apply a score cutoff and abstain below it."},{"title":"Guard","tag":"w06","md":"Run the input shield beside retrieval, spotlight the retrieved text, and check groundedness and citations before the answer leaves."},{"title":"Authorize","tag":"w07","md":"Resolve the caller from the token, live-check the restricted tier, scope caches and logs by ACL, and prove revocation with canaries."}]},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials"},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l1-pick","title":"Pick the RAG spine","prompt":"Flip the sources, the permission model and the corpus size. The ranking shows which spine fits and why it stops fitting.","dials":[{"id":"change","label":"Sources and change rate","values":[{"id":"batch","label":"Files in storage, changes daily"},{"id":"mixed","label":"Mixed sources, changes in minutes"},{"id":"msft","label":"Content stays in a system of record such as Microsoft 365"}]},{"id":"perm","label":"Permission model and revocation","values":[{"id":"groups","label":"Group ACLs, revocation in minutes"},{"id":"strict","label":"Per-document rules, revocation in seconds"},{"id":"open","label":"Everyone reads nearly everything"}]},{"id":"size","label":"Corpus size","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Under about 200,000 tokens, rarely changes"},{"id":"large","label":"Millions of chunks"}]}],"options":[{"id":"managed","name":"Managed indexers and integrated vectorization","summary":"Pull on a schedule, chunk and embed in the pipeline, pre-filter with security filters."},{"id":"custom","name":"Event-driven pipeline you own","summary":"Push on events with idempotent upserts, pre-filter, and live-check a restricted tier."},{"id":"delegate","name":"Delegate retrieval to the source","summary":"Query the system of record with the user's token and keep no copy of the content."},{"id":"context","name":"No index: long context or cache-augmented generation","summary":"Put the whole corpus in the prompt and cache the prefix."}],"scores":{"managed":{"change":{"batch":{"s":2,"why":"GA indexers read Blob Storage, SQL, Cosmos DB for NoSQL and OneLake on a 5 to 1,440 minute schedule with change and delete detection built in."},"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"Indexers cover only some sources and the SharePoint indexer is preview, so minute-level freshness needs push or events beside them."},"msft":{"s":-1,"why":"The SharePoint indexer is preview and copies content, so permissions and deletes become your sync problem."}},"perm":{"groups":{"s":1,"why":"Security filters (GA) or native ACL scopes (preview) pre-filter inside the index. Revocation lags by the sync interval."},"strict":{"s":-1,"why":"A scheduled sync cannot promise seconds, and the managed path has no live check to cover the gap."},"open":{"s":1,"why":"A tenant or public tag as a filter is cheap and there is little to maintain."}},"size":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"It works, but a pipeline and an index are more than a tiny corpus needs."},"large":{"s":1,"why":"Index size and filtered search scale on the service, and integrated vectorization handles chunking and embedding."}}},"custom":{"change":{"batch":{"s":-1,"why":"A queue, hashing and replay logic is more machinery than a daily file drop needs."},"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Storage events and change data capture arrive in seconds, at least once and out of order, so stable ids and idempotent upserts keep replays safe."},"msft":{"s":0,"why":"Graph change notifications work, but you own throttling, delta sync and permission capture."}},"perm":{"groups":{"s":1,"why":"You control how ACL fields land on every chunk and how fast membership changes propagate."},"strict":{"s":2,"why":"A pre-filter for the bulk plus a live check on the restricted tier gives seconds-level revocation where it matters."},"open":{"s":-1,"why":"Live checks and ACL sync are cost with no benefit when almost everything is visible."}},"size":{"small":{"s":-1,"why":"Operating a pipeline for a corpus that fits in a prompt is hard to justify."},"large":{"s":1,"why":"You can tune chunking, filters and the index for large corpora and skewed tenants."}}},"delegate":{"change":{"batch":{"s":-2,"why":"Files in storage have no query API that honours file permissions, so you would still build an index."},"mixed":{"s":-1,"why":"Each source needs its own connector and rate-limit handling, and ranking across sources is hard."},"msft":{"s":2,"why":"The source answers with its current permissions, so there is no copy, no ACL sync and no drift. Check its rate limits before you commit."}},"perm":{"groups":{"s":1,"why":"Permissions are honoured at query time with the user's token."},"strict":{"s":2,"why":"The source of truth decides every request, so revocation is as fast as the source."},"open":{"s":0,"why":"It works, but you pay source latency and quota for a rule that almost never denies."}},"size":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, with little to build, though a long prompt may be cheaper."},"large":{"s":0,"why":"Scale and ranking are the source's problem, so you control little of recall."}}},"context":{"change":{"batch":{"s":1,"why":"Rebuilding a cached prefix nightly is cheap when files change daily and the corpus is small."},"mixed":{"s":-2,"why":"Every change invalidates the prefix and forces a new prefill, so freshness costs money on each update."},"msft":{"s":-2,"why":"Permissioned content cannot sit in one shared prefix, and one prefix per user multiplies the cost."}},"perm":{"groups":{"s":-1,"why":"You must assemble a context per permission set and scope every cache entry to it."},"strict":{"s":-2,"why":"Per-document rules mean a different prompt per user, which defeats the shared prefix."},"open":{"s":1,"why":"One shared context works when everyone may read everything."}},"size":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Under about 200,000 tokens a knowledge base can go straight into the prompt, with no chunking, index or retrieval to fail."},"large":{"s":-2,"why":"Long contexts degrade well before the advertised limit, and cost scales with every request."}}}},"takeaway":"The spine follows the dials: managed for files and group ACLs, a pipeline you own when changes arrive in seconds or revocation is strict, delegation when the source already owns permissions, and no index at all when the corpus is small and open."},{"t":"h","text":"Failure modes that cross chapters","level":2,"id":"failure-modes-that-cross-chapters"},{"t":"failure","title":"Where one stage breaks the next","items":[{"symptom":"A revoked user still gets a snippet a day later","cause":"The index ACL syncs nightly and a semantic cache keyed on the question text holds the answer","fix":"Set a revocation SLA per tier, live-check the restricted tier, key caches by ACL scope, and run a nightly canary with a revoked test user."},{"symptom":"Part numbers never match while paraphrases work","cause":"Vector-only retrieval, or a generative query rewrite that drops the identifier","fix":"Add the keyword leg with hybrid search and RRF, and skip rewriting for identifier-shaped queries. Keep an identifier slice in the golden set."},{"symptom":"Callers with narrow access get empty or thin answers","cause":"A post-filter over a short candidate list, or a filtered vector search that returns too few rows","fix":"Pre-filter inside the index, check `vectorFilterMode`, raise the candidate count and track results per query by permission tier."},{"symptom":"Old policy text is still cited after the page changed","cause":"Timestamp change detection misses renames, restores and deletes, and child chunks of a replaced parent stay in the index","fix":"Hash content, use soft deletes or a change feed, delete by parent id and compare source and index counts daily."},{"symptom":"A fluent answer carries a citation that does not support the claim","cause":"A citation proves where text came from, not that it backs the claim","fix":"Add a groundedness check, a score cutoff with abstention, and a citation precision metric in the evaluation."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6","q":"Users report that questions containing part numbers such as ZX-4410-B return confident wrong answers, while paraphrased questions work. The manual is indexed. Which diagnosis fits this request best?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"vectorQueries\": [\n    { \"kind\": \"text\", \"text\": \"torque for ZX-4410-B flange bolts\", \"fields\": \"contentVector\", \"k\": 10 }\n  ],\n  \"queryType\": \"semantic\",\n  \"queryRewrites\": \"generative|count-3\",\n  \"top\": 5\n}"},"options":["The embedding model is too small, so move to the largest dimension size and re-embed every chunk with the new vectors","There is no keyword leg, and a generative rewrite can drop the identifier, so exact part numbers never match","The value of top is 5, so raising it to 50 will surface the right passage","Semantic ranking overwrites the vector order and hides the best chunk from the model"],"answer":1,"explain":"The request is vector-only with a rewrite on top. Azure AI Search recommends keyword search for product codes, dates and names because it finds exact matches, and the query rewrite feature (preview) can drop exact identifiers. Add the keyword leg with hybrid search and skip rewriting for identifier-shaped queries. A bigger embedding does not make a vector match an exact token, a larger top cannot return a chunk the vector leg never ranks, and the semantic ranker only reorders a shortlist of at most 50 results."},{"id":"w19-l1-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1","q":"You are moving a live index to a new embedding model. Put the steps in order.","items":["Create a second index with the new vector profile and the same ACL fields","Re-embed and load every chunk while dual-writing live changes to both indexes","Run the golden set and the access canaries against the new index","Repoint the alias to the new index","Keep the old index for a rollback window, then delete it"],"explain":"Vectors from different embedding models cannot share an index, and Azure embedding models cannot be upgraded in place, so the new index comes first. Dual-writing stops live changes from being lost during the backfill. Evaluation, including the access canaries because the new index is a fresh copy of every permission, must pass before cutover. The alias repoint is the atomic switch, and keeping the old index makes rollback a repoint."},{"id":"w19-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":2,"ref":"r18","q":"A contractor's access is revoked in the source system at 09:00. Select every place that can still serve them protected content after 09:00.","options":["Index ACL fields that the nightly sync has not refreshed","A group-membership cache with a 24-hour TTL","A semantic cache keyed on the question text only","The provider's prompt cache holding the KV state of a shared system prompt","Retrieval logs that store full chunk text"],"answers":[0,1,2,4],"explain":"A stale index ACL, a stale membership cache and a semantic cache with no ACL scope all return content under an old permission. Logs with full chunk text are a copy outside the ACL that an operator or a log search can read. The provider prompt cache holds model state for a repeated prefix and only helps a request that arrives with that prefix and valid authorisation, so it does not hand protected text to a revoked user. Its separate risk is timing probes across users, which is a different control."},{"id":"w19-l1-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"ref":"r10","q":"Your time to first token target drops from 3 s to 1.2 s. The request path runs serially: generative query rewrite, hybrid retrieval, semantic rerank, input attack shield, live permission check on the top hits, then generation. Which change recovers the most time without removing a control that stops leaks or injection?","options":["Drop the live permission check because the index filter already restricts results for every tier, including the restricted one","Remove the reranker and send the top 20 chunks so there is one stage less to wait for","Replace hybrid retrieval with a vector-only query so that one retrieval leg is saved on every single request","Run the shield beside retrieval and gate generation on its verdict, and skip the rewrite for identifier queries"],"answer":3,"explain":"The shield needs only the user text, so it can overlap with retrieval and still gate generation, and skipping the rewrite removes a whole model call on queries where it also hurts recall. Dropping the live check removes the only defence against a stale index ACL on the restricted tier. Removing the reranker lowers precision and lengthens the prompt, which costs time to first token elsewhere. The two retrieval legs already run in parallel, so going vector-only saves almost no time and loses exact matches."},{"id":"w19-l1-q5","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5","unit":"chunks","answer":5,"tolerancePct":5,"q":"Callers can read about 5% of the chunks, independent of relevance. An application post-filter fetches the top 100 candidates and must show 8 readable chunks. On average, how many readable chunks does it end up with?","explain":"100 candidates times 0.05 readable gives 5 chunks, fewer than the 8 you need. To expect 8 you must fetch 160 candidates, and every denied candidate has already reached the reranker, caches or logs. A pre-filter applies the rule inside the search, so the top k is full of readable chunks from the start. This is why Azure AI Search exposes `vectorFilterMode` with `preFilter` and `postFilter` and why selective access favours the pre-filter."},{"id":"w19-l1-q6","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2","q":"Match each symptom to the first place you look.","pairs":[{"l":"Old policy text is still quoted after the page changed","r":"Change and delete detection in ingestion"},{"l":"The answer cites the right document but the wrong paragraph","r":"Chunk boundaries and the mapping from citation to chunk id"},{"l":"A fluent claim that no retrieved passage supports","r":"The groundedness check and the abstention threshold"},{"l":"A user sees a snippet from a file they cannot open","r":"The access filter path, ACL sync and caches"},{"l":"A part number is never found but paraphrases work","r":"The keyword leg and the query rewrite settings"}],"explain":"Each symptom points to the stage that owns the promise. Ingestion owns deletes and renames, since the blob indexer tracks changes through LastModified and does not track deletes by default. Chunking owns what a citation can point at. Guards own unsupported claims. Access control owns who sees what. Retrieval owns exact matches."}],"defend":{"prompt":"Design a RAG assistant for a company whose knowledge sits in file shares, a wiki and a ticket system. It must answer employee questions with citations and respect each person's access rights. Walk me through ingest to answer, the options at each stage, what you would choose and how you would know it works. Two weeks after launch the CFO says part-number answers are wrong, and the security lead asks you to prove that a revoked contractor sees nothing. What do you do?","timebox":"7 min","clarify":["Which sources, how large is the corpus and how fast does each source change?","What do permissions look like (groups, per-document rules, deny entries, sharing links, guests) and how selective is access?","How fast must revocation take effect for each sensitivity tier?","What time to first token do users expect, and which questions are exact lookups versus paraphrases?","Is GA required in production or is preview acceptable for a pilot, and how many tenants are there?","Does the user's token reach retrieval, and what do caches and logs hold today?"],"options":[{"name":"Managed indexers, integrated vectorization and security filters (or native ACL scopes)","when":"Sources are files, SQL or Cosmos DB, permissions are group based and minutes of revocation lag is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Sync lag, and no live check for a restricted tier. Several connectors and the native ACL features are preview as of 2026-10-13."},{"name":"Event-driven pipeline you own, pre-filter plus live check on a restricted tier","when":"Mixed sources change in minutes, or revocation must take seconds for part of the corpus.","tradeoff":"You own idempotency, replay, throttling and ACL capture, so it needs a team that will operate it."},{"name":"Delegate retrieval to the source with the user's token (or agentic retrieval over knowledge sources, preview)","when":"Content lives in a system of record such as Microsoft 365 and permissions must be honoured at query time.","tradeoff":"Little control of ranking and filters, plus the source's latency and rate limits. Agentic retrieval planning is preview."},{"name":"No index: long context or cache-augmented generation","when":"The corpus is under about 200,000 tokens, changes rarely and almost everyone may read all of it.","tradeoff":"Cost per request, degraded recall in long contexts, and no clean way to enforce per-user permissions."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask about sources, corpus size, change rate, the permission model, revocation SLA per tier, the latency target, GA versus preview, and the mix of exact lookups and paraphrases."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. Options.** Four spines: managed indexers with integrated vectorization, an event-driven pipeline I own, delegation to the source, and the boring no-index option of long context or cache-augmented generation."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choice for assumed dials.** Assume 200,000 documents across a file lake, SharePoint and tickets, files that change daily and tickets that change in minutes, group-based ACLs, callers who see about 5% of the corpus, revocation in minutes for most content and seconds for a restricted tier, GA required, and a time to first token target under 2 s at p95. I would run a hybrid of the first two spines, landing in one Azure AI Search index:"},{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Ingest: a blob indexer on a short schedule for files, and storage events or change feeds for tickets pushed with `mergeOrUpload` and stable ids. Hash content to skip unchanged items, make deletes visible with tombstones, and send poison items to a dead-letter queue with replay.","Parse and chunk: layout analysis for PDFs, tables kept whole, splits along headings with a parent id, starting near 512 tokens with overlap and tuned on the golden set. Copy the ACL fields onto every chunk at ingest.","Retrieve: hybrid search with RRF so exact identifiers and paraphrases both match, the security filter as a pre-filter in the same query, the semantic ranker over the top 50, then a score cutoff with abstention.","Guard: Prompt Shields beside retrieval, spotlighted context, a groundedness check (preview) and citation enforcement on the output, and link and image stripping before rendering.","Authorize: the user's token end to end with on-behalf-of, group overage handled through Graph, fail closed on any error, a live check on the restricted tier, caches keyed by ACL scope with TTLs below the revocation SLA, and logs that hold ids and decisions, not chunk text.","Change safely: blue-green re-embedding behind an alias, with the golden set and access canaries gating the cutover."]},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip it.** Files only and no per-document permissions: the managed spine alone. Seconds-level revocation for everything in Microsoft 365: delegate to the source. Preview accepted: native ACL scopes replace my own group resolution. Under 200,000 tokens and open to all: skip the index."},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["Missed deletes or renames, stale text","Daily count and hash comparison of source against index, and the age of the oldest unsynced change"],["Identifier misses","An identifier slice in the golden set, with recall@k on exact-match queries"],["Filtered recall loss, thin answers for narrow-access users","Results per query by permission tier, and filtered recall against a brute-force baseline"],["ACL drift or revocation lag","Canary documents and a revoked test user in CI, and revocation lag at p95 per tier"],["Ungrounded answers or wrong citations","Groundedness and citation precision on a sampled stream, and the abstention rate"],["Injection through a document","Red-team documents with planted instructions, and egress alerts for unknown hosts"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** I would track recall@k and nDCG by slice, groundedness rate, citation precision, abstention rate, time to first token at p95, revocation lag at p95, ACL drift rate and cost per answered question. Rollout: one department behind a flag, shadow traffic against the golden set, canaries in CI, then widen."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"The two-week review","md":"**Part numbers.** Reproduce with the identifier slice. Check whether the request has a keyword leg and whether a rewrite dropped the identifier. Fix with hybrid search, skip rewriting for identifier-shaped queries and add those queries to the golden set. **Revoked contractor.** Show a canary: a document only the contractor could read, revoked at a known time. Assert that retrieval, the answer cache, the semantic cache, logs and traces return nothing after the SLA window, and show revocation lag at p95 from the run history."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about sources, change rate, permission model, revocation SLA, corpus size and latency before naming a tool","w":3},{"pt":"Offers at least three architectures, including the boring no-index or managed one, and picks for stated dials","w":3},{"pt":"Treats ingestion as a correctness stage: change and delete detection, stable ids, replay and ACL capture on every chunk","w":2},{"pt":"Combines keyword and vector retrieval and puts the access filter inside the query","w":2},{"pt":"Places guards with latency in mind: parallel input shield, groundedness check, citations and abstention","w":2},{"pt":"Handles identity and revocation: user token, group overage, live check, scoped caches and logs","w":3},{"pt":"Labels preview features (native ACLs, groundedness detection) and names the GA alternative","w":1},{"pt":"Names metrics by stage and a rollout with canaries, and answers the two-week review in order","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"One tenant holds half the corpus and keeps growing. What changes?","a":"Check filtered recall and index size under the skew. A dedicated index or partition for that tenant keeps its filter cheap and its blast radius small, while the rest stay in the shared index. Use an alias per index so re-embedding stays blue-green."},{"q":"Quality drops after you switch embedding models. How do you decide on rollback?","a":"Compare recall@k and nDCG by slice on both indexes, including the identifier and low-access slices. Because the alias still points at the old index until the evaluation passes, rollback is a repoint. Diagnose the slice that fell before the next attempt."},{"q":"Security refuses preview features in production. What do you give up?","a":"Native ACL scopes and token-based enforcement. You move to security filters, which are GA on any API version, and you own group resolution through Graph, overage handling and ACL updates. The canary, drift and cache rules stay the same."}],"redFlags":["Names a vector database before asking about permissions or update rate.","Puts the access rule in the system prompt, or filters after retrieval without mentioning starvation and denied text reaching rerankers, caches and logs.","Treats ingestion as a one-off load with no deletes, replays, idempotency or ACL capture.","Calls preview features GA, or offers no revocation test."]},"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r7","r8","r9","r10","r11","r12","r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","r18"],"takeaways":["Clarify sources, change rate, permission model, revocation SLA and latency before naming any tool.","Each stage hands the next a promise: ingest owns deletes and ACLs, chunking owns citations, retrieval owns exact matches, guards own unsupported claims, access control owns who sees what.","Pre-filter for the bulk, live-check the restricted tier, scope caches and logs, and test revocation with a canary on a schedule.","Say preview out loud: native ACL scopes and groundedness detection are preview as of 2026-10-13.","Replay the round with one dial changed: seconds-level revocation, then no permissions at all, and watch the spine change."]},{"id":"w19-l2","order":2,"title":"Boss: fast, cheap and available, from latency to caching, cost and resilience","kind":"boss","minutes":28,"brief":"An assistant has grown from a pilot to thousands of daily users. Leadership wants it to feel instant, cost less and survive a regional incident without breaking correctness or tenant isolation. Decide which lever fixes which problem and what each lever costs the others.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The interview prompt","level":2,"id":"the-interview-prompt"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["A chat assistant sends a long shared prefix (instructions, tool definitions, policies) plus retrieved context on every call","Users complain about the wait for the first word","Finance says the token bill keeps climbing","A regional incident took the assistant down for an hour"],"ask":["Is the wait the first token or the full answer, and what are the p50 and p95 targets?","How much of each prompt repeats across requests, and what changes inside it?","Is traffic steady or bursty, and how much work can wait for hours?","Do answers depend on the asking user's data or permissions, and are there tenant isolation rules?","Which models, regions and residency rules apply, and what recovery time and degraded quality are acceptable?","Where does the money go: uncached input, cached input, output or reasoning tokens?"],"tension":"Every lever buys speed or money with correctness, isolation or availability. Name what each one costs."},{"t":"h","text":"The request path","level":2,"id":"the-request-path"},{"t":"flow","title":"Fast, cheap and available on one path","steps":[{"title":"Gateway","tag":"w16","md":"Authenticate, apply per-consumer token limits, emit token metrics and route by backend health."},{"title":"Caches that cannot change the answer","tag":"w02","md":"Exact-match response cache first. A semantic cache only for answers that do not depend on the user, partitioned by scope."},{"title":"Router and output caps","tag":"w01","md":"Send simple requests to a smaller model, set reasoning effort per route and cap output tokens."},{"title":"A prompt that can hit the provider cache","tag":"w02","md":"Stable instructions and tools in a fixed order first, retrieved context next, the user turn last."},{"title":"Capacity","tag":"w16","md":"Provisioned throughput near the base load, a standard deployment for spillover, batch for work nobody waits on."},{"title":"Stream, fail over, degrade","tag":"w16","md":"Stream tokens. On failure retry once with jitter, spill over, trip the breaker, fail over to the second region, then step down a defined ladder."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l2-pick","title":"Pick the lever for the bottleneck","prompt":"Flip where the time goes, how traffic arrives and whether answers are private. The ranking shows the lever and what it risks.","dials":[{"id":"where","label":"Where it hurts","values":[{"id":"prefill","label":"First token is slow on long prompts"},{"id":"decode","label":"The whole answer is slow, outputs are long"},{"id":"queue","label":"429s and queueing at peak"}]},{"id":"traffic","label":"Traffic shape","values":[{"id":"steady","label":"Steady all day"},{"id":"bursty","label":"Sharp peaks, idle gaps"}]},{"id":"answers","label":"Answers","values":[{"id":"shared","label":"Identical across users"},{"id":"private","label":"Built from each user's data"}]}],"options":[{"id":"stream","name":"Stream, trim and cap the output","summary":"Show tokens as they arrive, shorten answers and set max output tokens."},{"id":"pcache","name":"Engineer the prompt for provider caching","summary":"Stable prefix first, fixed tool order, a cache key and a hit-rate alarm."},{"id":"small","name":"Route simple requests to a smaller model","summary":"Cheaper and faster per token, with a check that escalates hard cases."},{"id":"capacity","name":"Provisioned capacity with spillover","summary":"Reserved throughput for the base load, a standard deployment for overflow."},{"id":"scache","name":"Semantic cache at the gateway","summary":"Return a stored answer when a new prompt embeds close to an old one."}],"scores":{"stream":{"where":{"prefill":{"s":-1,"why":"Streaming and shorter answers do not shorten prefill, which is where the wait sits."},"decode":{"s":2,"why":"Output tokens dominate total time. Halving them can halve latency, and streaming shows words as they arrive."},"queue":{"s":0,"why":"Shorter outputs ease token limits a little, but queueing needs capacity."}},"traffic":{"steady":{"s":0,"why":"Output trimming works the same at any load."},"bursty":{"s":0,"why":"Output trimming works the same at any load."}},"answers":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"It does not touch correctness or isolation."},"private":{"s":0,"why":"It does not touch correctness or isolation."}}},"pcache":{"where":{"prefill":{"s":2,"why":"A cached prefix skips most of prefill, so time to first token falls and reads cost 0.1x on GPT-5.6 and later."},"decode":{"s":0,"why":"Caching does not shorten decoding."},"queue":{"s":0,"why":"Hits are cheaper, but a standard deployment still queues at its limit."}},"traffic":{"steady":{"s":1,"why":"Steady traffic keeps the prefix warm. Plan for about 15 requests a minute per prefix and key."},"bursty":{"s":-1,"why":"Idle gaps past the cache lifetime and bursts that spread over machines both cause misses."}},"answers":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Works the same, and the answer is always computed fresh."},"private":{"s":1,"why":"Keep user data out of the shared prefix and every answer is still computed fresh, so isolation holds."}}},"small":{"where":{"prefill":{"s":1,"why":"A smaller model also prefills faster."},"decode":{"s":2,"why":"A smaller model emits tokens faster and costs less per token."},"queue":{"s":1,"why":"More tokens per minute of quota per dollar, but not guaranteed capacity."}},"traffic":{"steady":{"s":1,"why":"A stable request mix is easy to evaluate and tune a router on."},"bursty":{"s":0,"why":"Routing quality does not depend on peaks."}},"answers":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Route on difficulty, not on who asks."},"private":{"s":0,"why":"Route on difficulty, not on whose data is used."}}},"capacity":{"where":{"prefill":{"s":0,"why":"Reserved throughput steadies latency but does not shorten prefill."},"decode":{"s":0,"why":"It does not shorten decoding."},"queue":{"s":2,"why":"Reserved capacity removes queueing from shared load, and spillover catches overflow on a standard deployment."}},"traffic":{"steady":{"s":2,"why":"Provisioned throughput is billed whether or not requests arrive, so it pays off on steady base load."},"bursty":{"s":0,"why":"Size for the base and let spillover absorb peaks, so bursts cost standard rates only while they last."}},"answers":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"No effect on correctness or isolation."},"private":{"s":0,"why":"No effect on correctness or isolation."}}},"scache":{"where":{"prefill":{"s":2,"why":"A hit skips the model, so there is no prefill and no decode."},"decode":{"s":2,"why":"A hit returns the stored answer at once."},"queue":{"s":1,"why":"Hits never reach the deployment, which relieves load."}},"traffic":{"steady":{"s":0,"why":"Hit rate depends on repeated questions, not on steadiness."},"bursty":{"s":1,"why":"Peaks repeat the same questions, and hits absorb them."}},"answers":{"shared":{"s":2,"why":"Identical questions deserve identical answers, so a hit is safe."},"private":{"s":-2,"why":"A hit can return one user's data to another unless you partition per user, which kills the hit rate."}}}},"takeaway":"Match the lever to the bottleneck first. Prompt caching and routing are cheap and reversible. A semantic cache is only safe for shared answers, and capacity pays off for steady base load."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Prompt cache read","value":"0.1x","note":"of the uncached input rate on GPT-5.6 and later. A write costs 1.25x."},{"label":"One write plus nine reads","value":"2.15x","note":"against 10x for ten uncached sends."},{"label":"Halve the output","value":"about 50%","note":"of latency. Halving the prompt gives about 1% to 5%."},{"label":"Batch discount","value":"50%","note":"with a 24-hour target. Not for interactive work."},{"label":"Minimum cacheable prefix","value":"1,024 tokens","note":"of visible input on GPT-5.6 and later."},{"label":"Foundry regional failover","value":"none automatic","note":"Approximate RTO: minutes hot/hot, 30 min to 2 h hot/warm, 2 to 8 h hot/cold."}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Where one lever breaks another","items":[{"symptom":"Cache hit rate collapses after a prompt change","cause":"A timestamp, user id or reordered tool list moved into the first 1,024 tokens, or traffic per prefix fell","fix":"Order content by how rarely it changes, pin the tool order, set `prompt_cache_key` per workflow and alert on the cached-token ratio."},{"symptom":"One user sees another user's answer","cause":"A semantic cache with no partition, or a threshold loose enough to match different questions","fix":"Partition with `vary-by`, keep the threshold strict (start near 0.05), and skip the cache for answers built from private data."},{"symptom":"A retry storm turns a brownout into an outage","cause":"Fixed sleeps, many attempts, SDK retries stacked on application retries, and retries on quota errors","fix":"Treat `Retry-After` as a minimum, add jitter, cap attempts and total time, count SDK retries and never retry quota or billing errors."},{"symptom":"The failover region returns 429 at once","cause":"No quota or capacity was reserved there, and quota is not a capacity guarantee","fix":"Size both regions for the load, drill the failover on a schedule and keep a degraded ladder for the gap."},{"symptom":"Cost climbs with every turn of a conversation","cause":"The full history is re-sent, or `previous_response_id` chaining bills the earlier input tokens again","fix":"Compact old turns, keep a stable prefix for caching and cap the turn count."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r19","unit":"%","answer":15.75,"tolerancePct":3,"q":"On GPT-5.6 and later models other than GPT-6.1 Sol a prefix is written once and read 19 more times inside its 30-minute lifetime. A write costs 1.25x and a read 0.1x the uncached input rate. What do the prefix tokens cost, as a percentage of 20 uncached sends?","explain":"One write is 1.25 and 19 reads are 19 x 0.1 = 1.9, so the total is 3.15 units against 20 for uncached sends. 3.15 / 20 = 0.1575, or 15.75%. The write premium is repaid after about two reads, which is why a prefix that is reused rarely or goes stale before it is read again can cost more than not caching."},{"id":"w19-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"ref":"r20","q":"A 6,200-token prompt is identical across users except for the question at the end, yet `cached_tokens` is always 0. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"messages = [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": f\"Request time: {request_time}\\nYou are the support assistant. <5,800 tokens of policy and tool notes>\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question},\n]\n# usage: prompt_tokens=6212, prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens=0"},"options":["A timestamp at the top of the system message changes the first 1,024 tokens on every request","The prompt is under the 1,024-token minimum, so caching never starts","The user question sits at the end of the prompt, and a prefix cache needs the question first","Prompt caching only works when streaming is turned off for the request"],"answer":0,"explain":"A cache hit needs an identical prefix, and on Azure before GPT-5.6 one differing character in the first 1,024 tokens gives `cached_tokens` of 0. The request time near the top breaks that. The prompt is 6,212 tokens, far above the minimum. The question belongs at the end, because the stable part must come first. Streaming has no effect on caching. Move the volatile line to the end."},{"id":"w19-l2-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"ref":"r23","q":"The FAQ bot uses an API Management semantic cache with `score-threshold` 0.05 and works well. The team adds HR questions whose retrieved context includes the asking employee's leave balance. What should change first?","options":["Lower the threshold to 0.02 so that only near-identical questions are able to hit the cache","Raise the threshold to 0.3 so that many more questions hit the cache and the monthly bill falls further","Partition the cache by user or permission scope with `vary-by`, or bypass it for private answers","Move the semantic cache behind the model so that it stores only generated text"],"answer":2,"explain":"The risk is not the match quality but who may see a stored answer. Two employees can ask the same words and need different answers, so the cache must be partitioned or skipped for private answers. A lower threshold still lets identical text from another user hit. A threshold above 0.2 can match different questions. The cache already sits in front of the model, so moving it does not change who can read an entry."},{"id":"w19-l2-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"ref":"r29","q":"Traffic is steady at about 400 requests per second for most of the day, with two 20-minute peaks at three times that. p95 latency has a target and the budget is capped. Which capacity plan fits best?","options":["Provisioned throughput sized for the peak of the two spikes, so no request ever spills to another deployment","Provisioned throughput near the base load, plus a standard deployment as spillover for the peaks","Standard pay-as-you-go only, with client-side retries and backoff to absorb the two daily peaks","Batch for every request, because Batch is 50% cheaper and the usage stays within its file limits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Provisioned capacity is billed whether or not requests arrive, so sizing for a three-times peak pays for idle capacity most of the day. Spillover sends non-200 overflow from the provisioned deployment to a standard one, so peaks cost standard rates only while they last. Standard only with retries gives unpredictable latency and retry waves at the peaks. Batch has a 24-hour target and cannot serve interactive requests."},{"id":"w19-l2-q5","type":"multi","tag":"bug","difficulty":3,"ref":"r31","q":"Select every defect in this retry loop for a 429 storm on an Azure OpenAI deployment.","code":{"lang":"python","code":"client = AzureOpenAI(max_retries=5)\n\nfor attempt in range(10):\n    try:\n        return client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=DEPLOYMENT, messages=msgs, max_tokens=4000)\n    except RateLimitError:\n        time.sleep(1)"},"options":["A fixed one-second sleep gives every client the same schedule, so retries arrive in waves","It ignores the `Retry-After` header that the service sends","Ten attempts with no total deadline, multiplied by the SDK's own `max_retries`, can mean about 60 calls","It retries at all, because a 429 is never a transient condition","`max_tokens=4000` inflates the up-front token estimate, so the call can be throttled below real usage"],"answers":[0,1,2,4],"explain":"No jitter and a fixed sleep synchronise clients, `Retry-After` should be the minimum wait, and stacked retry layers with no deadline multiply load on a service that is already struggling. The TPM check counts prompt plus `max_tokens` up front, so an oversized value consumes quota. Retrying a transient 429 is fine. The exception is a quota or billing error, which retrying cannot fix."},{"id":"w19-l2-q6","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":1,"ref":"r24","q":"Match each symptom to the lever that addresses it first.","pairs":[{"l":"First token takes 4 s on a 30,000-token prompt whose first 28,000 tokens never change","r":"Put the stable prefix first and rely on prompt caching"},{"l":"The first token is fast, but 700-token answers take 20 s to finish","r":"Trim and cap output tokens, and stream"},{"l":"One slow replica pushes p99 up while p50 is fine","r":"Hedge the request, using an idempotent call"},{"l":"429s appear at peak although quota looks unused","r":"Check the up-front token estimate and capacity throttling, then add spillover"},{"l":"A nightly enrichment job is billed at interactive prices","r":"Move it to Batch at 50% off with a 24-hour target"}],"explain":"Each lever matches one bottleneck. Prefill is attacked with a cacheable prefix, decode with fewer output tokens, tail latency with hedging, a 429 below quota by reading how the token estimate and capacity throttling work, and work that can wait with Batch. Reaching for the wrong lever, such as streaming to fix prefill, leaves the bottleneck where it was."}],"defend":{"prompt":"An internal assistant has grown from a pilot to 5,000 daily users. Leadership wants answers that feel instant, a smaller bill, and no outage when a region fails. How do you get there without breaking correctness or tenant isolation? Walk me through the options, what you do first and how you know it worked.","timebox":"6 min","clarify":["Is the wait the time to first token or the time to the full answer, and what are the p50 and p95 targets?","How much of each prompt repeats across requests, and what changes inside it?","Is traffic steady or bursty, and how much work is non-interactive and can wait?","Do answers depend on the asking user's data or permissions, and are there tenant isolation rules?","Which models, regions and residency rules apply, and what recovery time and degraded quality are acceptable?","What is the budget, and where is the money going: input, output or reasoning tokens?"],"options":[{"name":"Engineer the prompt and the call: stable prefix for provider caching, streaming, output caps and a router","when":"The bill and the wait come from long repeated prefixes and long outputs, and traffic keeps prefixes warm.","tradeoff":"A prompt refactor and a router to maintain and evaluate. Cache hits are best effort, with no guarantee."},{"name":"Add a gateway cache: exact match first, semantic cache for shared answers","when":"Many users ask the same questions and the answers do not depend on who asks.","tradeoff":"A hit can serve a wrong or another user's answer, so it needs a strict threshold, partitioning and an audit of false hits."},{"name":"Buy capacity: provisioned throughput for the base load, spillover to standard, Batch or Flex for work that can wait","when":"Traffic is steady with predictable peaks and p95 latency has a contract.","tradeoff":"Provisioned capacity is billed whether used or not, and quota is not a capacity guarantee. Flex is preview."},{"name":"Self-host open models with server-side prefix caching","when":"Residency, volume or control justify an inference team.","tradeoff":"You own GPUs, batching, scaling and on-call, and an open model may trail on quality."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** First token or full answer, repeated share of the prompt, traffic shape, private versus shared answers, residency and regions, acceptable degraded quality, and where the tokens go."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. Options.** Engineer the prompt and call, add a gateway cache, buy capacity, or self-host. They stack, so the question is the order."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choice for assumed dials.** Assume steady base load with two short 3x peaks, a 7,000-token shared prefix followed by retrieved private context, a p95 time to first token target of 2 s, an EU data zone with two allowed regions, a budget cut of 30%, and a fixed quality bar. I would:"},{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Measure first: split time to first token from total time, count tokens by category (uncached input, cached input, output, reasoning), and tag every request with route and tenant.","Fix the prompt for caching: stable instructions and tools in a fixed order, retrieved context next, the user turn last, a `prompt_cache_key` per workflow and an alert on the cached-token ratio. On GPT-5.6 and later a read costs 0.1x and a write 1.25x.","Cap the output at a measured percentile and ask for shorter answers, because output tokens dominate total time.","Route: a small model for classification, rewriting and simple lookups, and the strong model with a set reasoning effort for the rest. Reserve at least 25,000 tokens for reasoning and output on a reasoning model, add a cascade check and track quality per route.","Skip a semantic cache for private answers. Use an exact-match cache for identical requests, and a partitioned, strict semantic cache only for a public FAQ slice.","Capacity: provisioned throughput near the base load with spillover to a standard deployment, Batch for nightly enrichment, and token limits per consumer at the gateway.","Availability: retries with jitter that honour `Retry-After` under a total deadline, a circuit breaker per backend, a second region sized and drilled, and a degraded ladder of a smaller model, no tools, retrieval only with citations, then an honest error."]},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip it.** Unique prompts per request: caching does little, so routing and output caps carry the work. Bursty and low volume: skip provisioned throughput and use standard with spillover. Identical answers across users: add a semantic cache. Residency that forbids a second region: degrade inside the region and accept a longer recovery."},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["Cache hit rate collapses after a prompt change","Cached-token ratio per route, with an alert after every deploy"],["Semantic cache serves a wrong or foreign answer","Sampled hit audits, and a canary pair of users that must never share an answer"],["Retry storm or 429s below quota","Retries per success, 429 rate by cause, and the token estimate against actual use"],["Router degrades quality","Eval score and escalation rate per route, with a shadow comparison against the strong model"],["The second region cannot take the load","Scheduled failover drills that measure recovery time and error rate"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Time to first token at p50 and p95, total latency, cost per successful task (not per token), cached-token ratio, 429 and retry rate, availability against an SLO and quality by route. Shadow the router, canary the prompt reorder on a slice, and drill the failover before the next incident."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Order of operations","md":"Fix prompt order and output caps first: cheap, reversible and no new infrastructure. Then routing, then capacity, then a second region. Each later step costs more to build and to run, so state the order before you state the tools."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Splits time to first token from total time and finds the bottleneck before choosing a lever","w":3},{"pt":"Engineers the prompt for caching (stable prefix, fixed tool order, hit-rate metric) and knows the write and read prices","w":2},{"pt":"Treats a semantic cache as a correctness and isolation risk and scopes or skips it","w":3},{"pt":"Chooses capacity by traffic shape: provisioned for the base, spillover, Batch for work that can wait","w":2},{"pt":"Designs retries with jitter, deadlines and breakers, and a defined degradation ladder","w":3},{"pt":"Knows that quota is not capacity and that Foundry gives no automatic regional failover","w":2},{"pt":"Measures cost per successful task and tracks quality per route","w":2},{"pt":"Orders the work from cheap and reversible to expensive and operational","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"The cached-token ratio is 5% although the prefix is stable. What do you check?","a":"The 1,024-token minimum, anything volatile in the first tokens such as timestamps, ids or tool order, and the traffic per prefix: about 15 requests a minute per prefix and key before traffic can overflow to other machines and miss. Then compare the idle gaps with the cache lifetime."},{"q":"The CFO wants the semantic cache for HR answers. What do you say?","a":"Only for answers that are identical for every user. Partition with `vary-by`, keep the threshold strict (start near 0.05), audit hits for correctness and skip the cache for answers built from private data. Audits have also detected cross-user cache sharing through timing, so keep private data out of shared prefixes."},{"q":"The primary region fails. What happens in the first hour?","a":"Foundry does not fail over for you. Expect minutes with hot/hot, 30 minutes to 2 hours with hot/warm and 2 to 8 hours with hot/cold. The breaker trips, the degraded ladder starts, and the second region needs quota and capacity already sized."}],"redFlags":["Reaches for a semantic cache first, with no partitioning and no audit of false hits.","Optimises cost per token and never measures cost per successful task or quality per route.","Retries every error with fixed sleeps and no cap, or assumes quota means guaranteed capacity.","Plans a second region with no sizing, no quota and no failover drill."]},"refs":["r19","r20","r21","r22","r23","r24","r25","r26","r27","r28","r29","r30","r31","r32","r33","r34","r35","r36"],"takeaways":["Find the bottleneck first: first token and total time have different levers, and halving the output beats halving the prompt for total latency.","Prompt caching is an engineering task: stable prefix first, fixed tool order, a cache key and a hit-rate alarm. A semantic cache is a correctness and isolation risk.","Buy provisioned capacity for the base load and let spillover and Batch handle the rest. Quota is not capacity.","Retries need jitter, a deadline and a cap, and a second region needs sizing and a drill. Foundry does not fail over for you.","Order the work from cheap and reversible to expensive and operational, and measure cost per successful task."]},{"id":"w19-l3","order":3,"title":"Boss: the agent platform, tools, MCP, A2A, patterns and Agent Framework","kind":"boss","minutes":30,"brief":"Three teams want agents: one needs internal tools, one must hand work to a vendor's agent, and one runs an approval process that lasts days. Design the platform that connects them, with tools, peers, patterns and runtime, and every dial unspecified.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The interview prompt","level":2,"id":"the-interview-prompt"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["Team A's agent must call internal systems, some of them with side effects","Team B must delegate part of its work to an agent that a vendor runs","Team C runs a multi-step process with human approvals that can last days","Security wants one identity and one audit story"],"ask":["Who owns each capability: the same team, another team or a vendor?","Which calls are stateless lookups and which are long tasks that can pause for input?","Which actions write or cost money, and who approves them?","What identity does each call run under, and how do tokens flow between agent, tool server and downstream system?","How long can a run last, what must survive a restart, and is GA required?","Which languages, hosting and telemetry do the teams use already?"],"tension":"Standardising on one connection type is tidy and wrong. The seam you choose should follow ownership, state and risk."},{"t":"h","text":"The platform on one page","level":2,"id":"the-platform-on-one-page"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w19-l3-d1","title":"Layers of the agent platform","alt":"A host agent built with Agent Framework calls local functions, reaches shared internal tool servers through an MCP gateway, delegates to a vendor agent through an A2A client, and hands long approval work to a workflow with checkpoints where a person approves.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User or app] --> H[Host agent in Agent Framework]\n  H --> F[Local functions]\n  H --> G[MCP gateway]\n  G --> T[Internal tool servers]\n  H --> A[A2A client]\n  A --> V[Vendor agent]\n  H --> W[Workflow with checkpoints]\n  W --> P[Person approves]","caption":"Functions stay in process, MCP serves shared tools through a gateway, A2A crosses an ownership boundary, and the workflow owns waiting, approval and recovery."},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l3-pick","title":"Pick the seam","prompt":"Flip who owns the capability, the shape of the work and the risk of the action. The ranking shows which connection fits.","dials":[{"id":"owner","label":"Who owns the capability","values":[{"id":"same","label":"Same team, same codebase"},{"id":"other","label":"Another team in the company"},{"id":"vendor","label":"An outside vendor"}]},{"id":"shape","label":"Shape of the work","values":[{"id":"call","label":"A stateless call that returns data"},{"id":"task","label":"A task of minutes to days that may need input"}]},{"id":"risk","label":"Risk of the action","values":[{"id":"read","label":"Read-only and reversible"},{"id":"write","label":"Side effects that need approval and audit"}]}],"options":[{"id":"fn","name":"In-process function or agent as tool","summary":"Call it directly inside your own agent code."},{"id":"mcp","name":"MCP server behind a gateway","summary":"A shared tool contract with OAuth, a registry and audit."},{"id":"a2a","name":"A2A peer agent","summary":"Exchange tasks and messages with an opaque agent across a boundary."},{"id":"wf","name":"Workflow executor in your own graph","summary":"Put the step in a graph with checkpoints, approvals and resume."}],"scores":{"fn":{"owner":{"same":{"s":2,"why":"No network hop, no extra auth surface and no extra deployment. Agent Framework runs the tool loop."},"other":{"s":-1,"why":"Another team's code would ship inside your release and version with it."},"vendor":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot import a vendor's agent. You need a protocol boundary."}},"shape":{"call":{"s":1,"why":"A stateless call is the simplest thing a function does."},"task":{"s":-1,"why":"A long task blocks the loop unless you add state, polling and recovery yourself."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Read-only, reversible calls need little ceremony."},"write":{"s":0,"why":"Side effects work, but approvals and audit are yours to add."}}},"mcp":{"owner":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"A server for one team's own tools adds a hop for little reuse."},"other":{"s":2,"why":"One contract serves many agents and teams, behind a gateway with OAuth and a curated catalog."},"vendor":{"s":1,"why":"A vendor's MCP server works for tools and data, with token audience, passthrough and poisoning risks to manage."}},"shape":{"call":{"s":2,"why":"Tools and resources fit stateless lookups, with typed schemas."},"task":{"s":0,"why":"Multi round-trip requests and the Tasks extension allow long work, but MCP is not a peer-agent protocol."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Annotations can mark a tool read-only, though clients must treat them as untrusted hints."},"write":{"s":1,"why":"OAuth 2.1, resource indicators and a gateway give per-tool authorisation and audit, if you refuse token passthrough."}}},"a2a":{"owner":{"same":{"s":-2,"why":"A network hop and an Agent Card for code you could call in process."},"other":{"s":1,"why":"A peer with its own roadmap and task lifecycle fits once calls are more than lookups."},"vendor":{"s":2,"why":"Opaque execution: the vendor keeps prompts, tools and state private, and you exchange tasks and messages."}},"shape":{"call":{"s":-1,"why":"A task with states is heavy for a stateless lookup."},"task":{"s":2,"why":"Task states, INPUT_REQUIRED, streaming and push notifications are built for long, interruptible work."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":0,"why":"A2A does not change the risk of a read."},"write":{"s":1,"why":"A separate trust boundary with its own auth contains the blast radius, but AUTH_REQUIRED is not authorisation by itself."}}},"wf":{"owner":{"same":{"s":1,"why":"A graph you own gives a fixed order, retries and typed messages between steps."},"other":{"s":-1,"why":"Another team's step is better behind a contract than inside your graph."},"vendor":{"s":-2,"why":"A vendor's system cannot be an executor in your code."}},"shape":{"call":{"s":-1,"why":"A graph for one call adds structure you do not need."},"task":{"s":2,"why":"Supersteps, checkpoints, pending requests and resume survive crashes and long waits."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":0,"why":"Reads need no gate."},"write":{"s":2,"why":"Human-in-the-loop requests are saved in checkpoints, and idempotent side effects make replays safe."}}}},"takeaway":"Functions for your own lookups, MCP for shared tools, A2A across an ownership boundary, and a workflow when the work waits, needs approval or must survive a crash."},{"t":"table","caption":"Climb the ladder only when a dial forces you","head":["Level","Climb here when","What it costs"],"rows":[["Direct call or plain function","The task is one step, or you can write the function","No autonomy, which is often fine"],["Workflow: chain, route, parallelize","The steps are known and the order matters","Someone writes and maintains the graph"],["Single agent with tools","The next step depends on what the model finds","Loops need step, token and time budgets"],["Harness Agent","Long multi-step work needs todos, plan and execute modes, approvals and history persistence","More defaults to understand and approve"],["Multi-agent: supervisor, handoff, group chat, Magentic","Breadth beyond one context, tool overload or isolation","About 15x chat tokens in one published research system, and conflicting parallel writes"]]},{"t":"failure","title":"Platform failure modes across chapters","items":[{"symptom":"Tool selection gets worse after the fourth MCP server","cause":"Definitions crowd the context: a typical five-server setup uses about 55k tokens and selection degrades past 30 to 50 tools","fix":"Curate a bundle per agent, load tools on demand with tool search, and keep the `tools/list` order deterministic so caching still hits."},{"symptom":"A reviewed tool starts leaking data after an update","cause":"A rug pull or poisoned description: tool text is model input and the server can change it","fix":"Pin and diff definitions, show changes to a person, run tools with least privilege and treat annotations as untrusted."},{"symptom":"A downstream API accepts a call it should reject","cause":"The MCP server forwarded the inbound token, which was never issued for that API: token passthrough and a confused deputy","fix":"Validate the audience, exchange tokens with on-behalf-of for downstream calls and never forward the inbound token."},{"symptom":"Agents pass the user back and forth until the budget is gone","cause":"A handoff mesh with no turn cap","fix":"Cap turns, set token and time budgets in the harness and log every transfer."},{"symptom":"A resumed run sends the email twice","cause":"A checkpoint restores state, but the side effect had already happened after it","fix":"Put an idempotency key on every write, record intent before acting and compensate where no key exists."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"ref":"r40","q":"What is wrong with this MCP tool handler?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"@server.tool()\nasync def get_invoice(invoice_id: str, ctx):\n    token = ctx.request.headers[\"Authorization\"]  # issued to this MCP server\n    resp = await http.get(f\"{BILLING_API}/invoices/{invoice_id}\",\n                          headers={\"Authorization\": token})\n    return resp.json()"},"options":["Nothing: forwarding the caller's token keeps the user's identity intact from the client all the way to the billing API and its audit log","It should return an MCP resource instead of JSON, because tools are not allowed to read data","It forwards a token issued for this MCP server to the billing API: token passthrough, a confused deputy risk","A tool handler must never call an HTTP API, so the billing lookup has to run in the host"],"answer":2,"explain":"An MCP server must not accept or forward a token that was not issued for it. The client asked for a token with this server as the resource, so the billing API is the wrong audience and should reject it. Use an on-behalf-of or token-exchange step to obtain a token for the billing resource. Tools may return data and may call HTTP APIs, and the host does not need to run the lookup."},{"id":"w19-l3-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"ref":"r43","q":"Select every sound reason to reach a remote agent over A2A instead of wrapping it as an MCP tool.","options":["The remote agent belongs to a vendor and must keep its prompts, tools and state private","The work takes minutes and can pause in INPUT_REQUIRED for a person's reply","The caller needs streamed progress or a push notification when the task finishes","The remote capability is a single stateless lookup that returns JSON","You want your model to see the remote agent's own tool list and prompt templates"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"A2A is built for opaque peers, long and interruptible tasks, and streaming or push updates. A stateless JSON lookup is a tool or a function, and a plain MCP tool is simpler. Exposing the remote agent's tools and prompts defeats the opacity that A2A preserves. MCP and A2A are complementary, so a remote agent may well use MCP tools internally."},{"id":"w19-l3-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"ref":"r50","q":"A workflow pauses for a manager's approval, then the host process crashes. Put the recovery in order.","items":["The approval executor emits a request and the superstep ends, so a checkpoint is written","The host process crashes while the request is pending","A new process restores the latest checkpoint from durable storage","The pending approval request is re-emitted to the caller","The manager answers and the caller resumes with `workflow.run(responses=...)`","Downstream executors continue from the next superstep"],"explain":"A checkpoint is written at the end of each superstep and holds executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses and shared state. Because the request is stored in the checkpoint, a restore re-emits it and the answer resumes the run. In-memory storage does not survive the crash, so the storage must be durable. Any side effect after the last checkpoint is repeated on resume, which is why writes need idempotency keys."},{"id":"w19-l3-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":3,"ref":"r46","q":"You chose parallel read-only sub-agents for a research question. The task changes to editing one codebase where each change depends on the others. What flips?","options":["One agent doing single-threaded writes, with other agents limited to read-only review","More parallel writer agents, each owning one file, with a supervisor that merges all of their results at the end","A group chat of peers that debate every change before one of them commits it","A Magentic manager with a task ledger so that it can keep several writers aligned"],"answer":0,"explain":"Research reads parallelise well: Anthropic's multi-agent research system beat a single agent by 90.2% on an internal eval, at about 15x the tokens of chat. Tightly coupled writes are different, because each parallel writer makes implicit decisions that conflict with the others, which is Cognition's argument against parallel multi-agent designs. Keep writes single-threaded and use extra agents to review. A supervisor merge, a debate or a ledger add coordination cost without removing the conflicting decisions."},{"id":"w19-l3-q5","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r41","unit":"tokens","answer":561000,"tolerancePct":1,"q":"Five MCP servers add 55,000 tokens of tool definitions to every model call. An agent run makes 12 model calls. Tool search cuts the definitions by 85%. How many input tokens per run does it save?","explain":"55,000 x 0.85 = 46,750 tokens saved per call, and 46,750 x 12 = 561,000 tokens per run. The money saved is smaller if the stable definitions already hit a prompt cache at 0.1x, but selection accuracy still improves, because tool selection degrades past 30 to 50 tools. Tool search changes what the model sees, so keep the `tools/list` order deterministic or the cache hits disappear."},{"id":"w19-l3-q6","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"ref":"r42","q":"A client follows up on a finished A2A task and gets the error below. What is the right fix?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"SendMessage message{ messageId: m-9, role: ROLE_USER, taskId: t-41, contextId: c-7,\n  parts: [ text: \"Shorten the summary to two sentences\" ] }\n-> rejected: task t-41 is in a terminal state (TASK_STATE_COMPLETED)"},"options":["Retry with the same taskId repeatedly until the server finally agrees to reopen the finished task","Generate a fresh taskId on the client side and send the follow-up message using that new id","Switch the binding from JSON-RPC to gRPC, because gRPC is the binding that lets terminal tasks restart","Start a new task: send the message in the same contextId with no taskId (optionally `referenceTaskIds`)"],"answer":3,"explain":"COMPLETED, FAILED, CANCELED and REJECTED are terminal, and a terminal task cannot restart. A refinement is a new task in the same context, which may point to the earlier one with `referenceTaskIds`. Task ids are generated by the server, so the client cannot supply one for a new task. The three bindings are functionally equivalent, so changing it does not change task rules."}],"defend":{"prompt":"Three teams want agents. One needs to call internal systems, one has to delegate part of its work to an agent a vendor runs, and one runs an approval process that can take days and must survive restarts. Design the platform: how tools are exposed, how agents talk to each other, which patterns you allow and what runtime you standardise on. Walk me through the options and your choice, and tell me what you would refuse to standardise.","timebox":"7 min","clarify":["Who owns each capability and agent: the same team, another team or a vendor?","Which calls are stateless lookups and which are long tasks that can pause for input?","Which actions write or cost money, and who approves them?","What identity does each call run under, and how do tokens flow between agent, tool server and downstream system?","How long can a run last, what must survive a restart, and is GA required?","Which languages, hosting and telemetry do the teams use already?"],"options":[{"name":"Functions and agents-as-tools inside one Agent Framework codebase","when":"One team, one codebase, and the calls are lookups.","tradeoff":"Does not cross an ownership boundary, and reuse means copying code."},{"name":"MCP servers behind a gateway, with a curated catalog","when":"Several agents or teams share tools and you need one contract, OAuth and audit.","tradeoff":"A gateway to run, the token cost of definitions, and tool poisoning and token passthrough to police."},{"name":"A2A peers across ownership boundaries","when":"A vendor or another team runs an opaque agent and the work is long and can need input.","tradeoff":"An Agent Card, auth and task lifecycle to operate, and a network hop on every exchange."},{"name":"A workflow with checkpoints and approvals for the long process","when":"The steps are known, waits last days and side effects need approval.","tradeoff":"You design the graph, durable storage and idempotency. The Durable Extension is Beta or preview as of 2026-10-13."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** Ownership of each capability, stateless versus long tasks, side effects and approvers, the identity and token path, run length and restart needs, GA versus preview, and the stacks the teams use."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. Options.** In-process functions, MCP servers behind a gateway, A2A peers, and a workflow with checkpoints. They are layers, not rivals."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choice for assumed dials.** Assume teams on Agent Framework in .NET and Python, internal tools shared by teams A and C, a vendor-owned agent, an approval process lasting up to a week, writes that need approval and audit, and Entra-issued OAuth tokens. I would standardise on layers, not on one connection type:"},{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Start each agent as one agent with tools and climb only when a named dial forces it: a workflow for known steps, a Harness Agent for long open-ended work, multi-agent only for breadth, tool overload or isolation. If a function can do the job, use the function.","In-process functions for a team's own lookups, which add no auth surface.","Shared internal tools as MCP servers behind a gateway: OAuth 2.1 with protected resource metadata and resource indicators, no token passthrough (on-behalf-of for downstream calls), a curated catalog per agent, tool search when definitions take more than a few percent of the context, a deterministic `tools/list` order so caching still hits, and annotations treated as untrusted hints.","The vendor agent as an A2A peer: read its Agent Card, authenticate with the scheme it declares, treat AUTH_REQUIRED as a request for credentials and never as consent, and run long work as a task with streaming or push.","The approval process as a workflow: an executor per step, checkpoints on durable storage and not in memory, human-in-the-loop requests for approvals, tool approval bound to the same session for risky tools, and an idempotency key on every side effect because a restore replays from the last checkpoint.","Budgets in the harness: a step cap, token and time budgets, a turn cap on handoffs and a stop condition for every loop.","One telemetry convention: OpenTelemetry GenAI spans from Agent Framework, with trace context propagated across MCP and A2A calls."]},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip it.** One team and one codebase: functions and a single agent, with no MCP and no A2A. A vendor that offers only an MCP server: use MCP for its tools and A2A only if it runs an agent. Runs under an hour with no approvals: skip the workflow and its durable storage. GA only: check the status of every checkpoint store and hosting package, since some are Beta or preview as of 2026-10-13."},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["Tool poisoning or a rug pull","Hash and diff tool definitions on every connect, and alert on any change"],["Token passthrough or a wrong audience","Gateway checks of audience and issuer, and a deny test with a token issued for another resource"],["Handoff or agent loops","Turns per run, repeated state hashes and budget-exceeded events"],["Duplicate side effects after a resume","A chaos test that kills the process after each superstep and asserts one effect per idempotency key"],["Tool overload","Tool-selection accuracy on an eval slice, and definition tokens as a share of the context"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Task success rate, cost per successful task, steps per run, tool-call error rate, approval latency, resume success after a forced crash, and definition tokens as a share of context. Roll out one team on the gateway with read-only tools, then writes with approval, then the vendor peer, with a kill switch per tool and per agent."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What I refuse to standardise","md":"One connection type for everything, an uncurated tool catalog, and any multi-agent topology that no named dial forced. Each of them trades a visible convenience for a hidden cost in tokens, risk or debuggability."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about ownership, task length, side effects, identity and GA before choosing protocols","w":3},{"pt":"Places each seam by ownership, state and risk: functions, MCP, A2A and workflow, not one for all","w":3},{"pt":"Applies MCP authorization correctly: protected resource metadata, resource indicators and no token passthrough","w":2},{"pt":"Explains why A2A suits opaque, long, interruptible work and how it differs from MCP","w":2},{"pt":"Starts with one agent or a workflow and names the dial that justifies each added agent","w":2},{"pt":"Designs the long process for recovery: durable checkpoints, human requests and idempotent side effects","w":3},{"pt":"Sets budgets, turn caps and stop conditions, and names loop and tool-catalog failure modes","w":2},{"pt":"Labels preview or Beta pieces and gives metrics and a rollout","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"The vendor's agent answers AUTH_REQUIRED in the middle of a task. What does your client do?","a":"Treat it as a request for credentials or consent, not as authorization. Obtain the credential through the declared security scheme, ideally with a person in the loop, and continue the same task. The transition itself never approves an operation."},{"q":"Tool definitions take 15% of the context. What do you change?","a":"Curate a smaller bundle per agent, load tools on demand with tool search, shorten descriptions and keep the `tools/list` order deterministic so caching works. Anthropic reports selection degrading past 30 to 50 tools and a five-server setup near 55k tokens."},{"q":"How do you stop two agents from editing the same file?","a":"Give writes to one agent, single-threaded, and make the others read-only reviewers. Parallel writers make conflicting implicit decisions. If you must parallelise, partition ownership by file and merge through one writer."}],"redFlags":["Proposes A2A or multi-agent for everything, with no dial that forced it.","Forwards the user's token through the MCP server, or treats tool annotations as a security control.","Keeps workflow checkpoints in memory for a process that waits for days, or retries side effects with no idempotency.","Gives agent loops no step, token or turn budget."]},"refs":["r17","r37","r38","r39","r40","r41","r42","r43","r44","r45","r46","r47","r48","r49","r50","r51"],"takeaways":["Choose the seam by ownership, state and risk: functions inside a team, MCP for shared tools, A2A across a boundary, a workflow when the work waits or needs approval.","Never forward a token that was not issued for your server. Exchange it, and treat tool annotations and AUTH_REQUIRED as signals, not as permission.","Climb from a function to a workflow to one agent to many agents only when a named dial forces it, and put budgets on every loop.","Make long runs recoverable: durable checkpoints, human-in-the-loop requests and idempotency keys on every side effect.","Curate the tool catalog, keep definitions stable and watch definition tokens as a share of the context."]},{"id":"w19-l4","order":4,"title":"Shipping to the enterprise: Foundry hosted agents, identity, Agent 365 and governance","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer hands you a framework-built crew, some Copilot Studio agents and a partner agent, and asks for one governed path to production. Every dial is open: ask, compare, choose, name the failures and say how you will prove it is safe to widen.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The setup: a fleet, a deadline and a kill switch","level":2,"id":"the-setup-a-fleet-a-deadline-and-a-kill-switch"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's setup","known":["A crew built on Microsoft Agent Framework runs a long, approval-gated process","Some Copilot Studio agents and one partner-built agent already exist","Leadership wants them in Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot, each with a named owner, an audit trail and a way to switch it off within the hour"],"ask":["Does each agent act for a signed-in user, on a schedule, or as a coworker in group chats?","How reversible and how external are the actions, and who may approve them?","Can an approval wait for days, and must the wait survive a restart?","Must any code stay off Azure, and who owns the partner agent's code?","Which previews does the compliance bar allow, and which EU AI Act dates apply to us?"],"tension":"Speed pulls toward one broad identity and a hosted agent for everything. Accountability pulls toward narrow identities, tiered approvals and a kill switch that somebody has rehearsed."},{"t":"h","text":"From framework code to a governed fleet","level":2,"id":"from-framework-code-to-a-governed-fleet"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w19-l4-fleet-path","title":"The path every agent walks","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent Framework crew] --> B[Hosted or self-hosted runtime]\n  B --> C[Blueprint, agent identity, sponsor]\n  C --> D[Toolbox and Foundry IQ]\n  D --> E[Publish to Teams and Copilot]\n  E --> F[Agent 365 registry]\n  F --> G[Traces, Purview audit, Defender]\n  G --> H[Review, kill switch, retire]","alt":"A left to right flow: Agent Framework crew, runtime, identity with sponsor, tools and knowledge, publishing, registry, telemetry and audit, then review and retirement.","caption":"Chapters 11, 12 and 17 meet on this path. Skipping a step leaves a gap that shows up as an unowned or unobserved agent."},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials across three chapters","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials-across-three-chapters"},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l4-runtime-and-brake","title":"Pick the runtime for the dials you were given","prompt":"Flip code needs, action risk and the status bar. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"code","label":"Code and state needs","values":[{"id":"config","label":"Configuration is enough"},{"id":"custom","label":"Custom orchestration, files, long approvals"},{"id":"offsite","label":"Code must stay off Azure"}]},{"id":"risk","label":"Action risk","values":[{"id":"read","label":"Read-only answers"},{"id":"write","label":"Reversible internal writes"},{"id":"ext","label":"External or irreversible actions"}]},{"id":"bar","label":"Status bar","values":[{"id":"ga","label":"GA features only"},{"id":"preview","label":"Named previews allowed"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prompt","name":"Prompt agent with toolbox","summary":"Configuration only. Foundry runs it, tools sit behind one managed endpoint."},{"id":"hosted","name":"Hosted agent on Agent Framework","summary":"Your code in per-session sandboxes, workflows with checkpoints and approvals."},{"id":"self","name":"Self-hosted and registered","summary":"AKS or Container Apps, joined to governance by SDK or registry sync."},{"id":"studio","name":"Copilot Studio agent","summary":"Low-code studio with channel reach, listed in the Agent 365 registry."}],"scores":{"prompt":{"code":{"config":{"s":2,"why":"Nothing to build or run, and tracing is GA for prompt agents."},"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"No custom code path for per-session files or checkpointed workflows."},"offsite":{"s":-2,"why":"It runs inside Foundry, so code and data cannot stay elsewhere."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Read-only answers need little beyond guardrails and traces."},"write":{"s":0,"why":"Write gates rest on tool-level controls, so tiering stays coarse."},"ext":{"s":-1,"why":"No custom approval logic or checkpointed wait for a reviewer."}},"bar":{"ga":{"s":1,"why":"The core agent surface is GA, the closest fit to a GA-only bar."},"preview":{"s":0,"why":"Previews such as tool search add little here."}}},"hosted":{"code":{"config":{"s":-1,"why":"Overbuilt: you maintain code and pay per active session for what configuration covers."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Your orchestration, `$HOME` state and per-session sandboxes."},"offsite":{"s":-2,"why":"Code runs in Foundry sandboxes, so it cannot meet a must-stay-elsewhere rule."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":0,"why":"It works, but session cost exceeds the need."},"write":{"s":1,"why":"Tool approval and middleware live in code you own."},"ext":{"s":2,"why":"A workflow parks a human request in a checkpoint and binds the approval to it."}},"bar":{"ga":{"s":-1,"why":"The GA table had no hosted agent row on 2026-10-13 and the Agent Framework hosting package is Beta."},"preview":{"s":1,"why":"Acceptable with a dated waiver and a redeploy plan after the 2026-08-20 backend change."}}},"self":{"code":{"config":{"s":-2,"why":"You would run scaling, identity wiring and patching for no gain."},"custom":{"s":1,"why":"Full control, but you supply session persistence and isolation."},"offsite":{"s":2,"why":"Code and data stay where the rules demand, then you register the agent."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":-1,"why":"Heavy for read-only answers."},"write":{"s":1,"why":"Your middleware and approvals, registered for governance."},"ext":{"s":1,"why":"Same approval design as hosted, plus your own audit store."}},"bar":{"ga":{"s":1,"why":"You choose GA components and join Agent 365 by SDK or registry sync."},"preview":{"s":0,"why":"No preview is needed, and none adds value here."}}},"studio":{"code":{"config":{"s":2,"why":"Low-code authoring and channel reach with no code to host."},"custom":{"s":-1,"why":"Low-code limits custom orchestration and per-session files."},"offsite":{"s":-1,"why":"A managed studio is no home for code that must stay elsewhere."}},"risk":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Fine for read-only help with broad channel reach."},"write":{"s":0,"why":"Connectors and approvals are configured, not coded."},"ext":{"s":-1,"why":"Less room for custom, checkpointed review steps."}},"bar":{"ga":{"s":0,"why":"Check each feature's status, as the app creation experience was preview on 2026-10-13."},"preview":{"s":1,"why":"Preview studio features are usable with a waiver after checking each one."}}}},"takeaway":"Pick the smallest runtime that meets the code and risk dials, then let the status bar overrule an elegant choice. Governance joins every runtime, so it is never the reason to pick one."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Someone accountable for every agent, a kill switch you have timed, and approvals that are bound, tiered and fail closed. Date-stamp the previews (Agentic-User needs the Frontier program, discovery of local and shadow agents was announced as preview on 2026-05-01) and name the retirements (Workflows 2026-12-01, classic agents 2027-03-31)."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An agent calls a storage service through a toolbox and gets 403, although its RBAC role is correct. The decoded token is below. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"aud\": \"https://mcp.example.com\",\n  \"sub\": \"3f2c0e1a-user\",\n  \"scp\": \"user_impersonation\",\n  \"azp\": \"agent-identity-client-id\",\n  \"ver\": \"2.0\"\n}"},"options":["On-behalf-of tokens cannot reach storage, so the agent must switch to the Agentic-User mode","The agent identity has no sponsor, so Entra strips the delegated scopes from every token it issues","The audience is the MCP server URL, not the downstream resource id, so storage rejects the token","The blueprint holds the credentials, so an agent identity cannot obtain tokens for other services"],"answer":2,"explain":"The `aud` claim names the MCP server. A downstream service accepts a token only when the audience is its own resource id, for example `https://storage.azure.com`, so it returns 403 even when RBAC is correct. Decode the token and compare `aud` first. OBO is the right mode for acting as the user, and the subject is the user as expected. A missing sponsor affects lifecycle control, not token contents. Blueprints hold credentials by design, and agent identities still get tokens through them.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l4-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which controls keep an approval gate from being bypassed? Choose all that apply.","options":["Keep the default binding, so an approval response is accepted only for a pending request in the same session","Tier by action class and arguments, so a second tool with the same effect lands in the same tier","Set `disable_tool_auto_approval=True` so every call in the process needs approval","Fail closed, so an unanswered request means the action does not run","Attach approval to one named tool and rely on its description to keep the model on that path"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Binding stops fabricated or replayed approvals, tiering by class and arguments closes alternative paths, and failing closed removes the timeout loophole. `disable_tool_auto_approval=True` removes the Harness approval middleware and does not remove the requirement from a tool marked always_require, so it does not make every call need approval. Gating one named tool leaves another tool free to cause the same effect.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l4-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the governance lifecycle of an agent in order.","items":["Register the agent in the inventory before it deploys","Give it its own identity and a named sponsor","Grant least privilege and add Conditional Access","Review purpose, access and behavior on a schedule","Retire it: remove identity, credentials, memory, data and the registry entry"],"explain":"An unlisted agent is an ungoverned agent, so inventory comes first. Identity and a sponsor must exist before permissions go to something accountable. Review repeats on a schedule, and retirement removes every trace so no orphaned credential or memory remains.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w19-l4-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"An approval-gated workflow on self-hosted Agent Framework may wait several days for a reviewer. Which design keeps the wait cheap and restart-safe?","options":["Raise a human request from the workflow, persist checkpoints to storage and resume with the response","Run the wait as a background agent, which the framework restarts automatically if the host process dies","Keep the agent loop open and poll the reviewer queue every few seconds until someone answers it","Apply compaction so the pending request stays small and never has to leave the in-memory history"],"answer":0,"explain":"Workflow checkpoints store pending requests and re-emit them on restore, so a restart loses nothing and the wait holds no compute. Background agents are experimental and in-flight work does not survive a restart. Polling holds a process and a loop for days. Compaction trims in-memory history and adds no durability. The Durable Extension can pause for input at no compute or token cost, but its Python packages are Beta and its .NET packages are preview as of 2026-10-13.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l4-q5","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A hosted agent has sustained peaks of 0.6 vCPU and 1.6 GiB of memory. Docs advise moving to the next size when sustained peaks pass about 70 percent of allocation. Sizes are 0.5 vCPU with 1 GiB, 1 vCPU with 2 GiB and 2 vCPU with 4 GiB. What is the smallest size, in vCPU, that keeps both peaks at or under 70 percent?","answer":2,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"vCPU","hint":"Check CPU and memory against each size.","explain":"At 0.5 vCPU the CPU peak is 120 percent. At 1 vCPU with 2 GiB the CPU peak is 60 percent but memory is 80 percent, so memory forces the next size. At 2 vCPU with 4 GiB the peaks are 30 and 40 percent. Billing follows CPU plus memory across active sessions, so right-size the next version rather than over-provisioning.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l4-q6","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"The agent must react to event triggers with no signed-in user, post in group chats and act as itself in Microsoft 365. Which identity setup fits, given the 2026-10-13 status?","options":["OBO with the token of whoever created the chat, so every action still follows a real person's permissions","S2S with application permissions, because app-only tokens are allowed to perform Microsoft 365 actions","A shared service account with a long-lived secret held in Key Vault and reused by every agent","Agentic-User: an agent user account beside the agent identity, which needs the Frontier preview"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without an agent user, Microsoft 365 actions happen only on behalf of a signed-in user. A group chat has no single user whose permissions should apply, so OBO misattributes actions and can expose content to members who cannot see it. App-only S2S cannot perform Microsoft 365 actions. A shared secret widens the blast radius and gives up per-agent accountability. The cost is that Agentic-User is Frontier preview, so record it as a dated exception.","difficulty":3}],"defend":{"prompt":"We have a crew built on Microsoft Agent Framework, a few Copilot Studio agents and one partner-built agent. Leadership wants all of them in Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot this quarter, each with a named owner, an audit trail and a way to switch any one off within an hour. Design the path from framework code to a governed fleet.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Does each agent act for a signed-in user, on a schedule or as a coworker in group chats?","How reversible and how external are the actions, and who is allowed to approve them?","Can an approval wait for days, and must the wait survive a restart or a redeploy?","Must any code or data stay off Azure, and who owns the partner agent's code?","Which previews does the compliance bar allow, and is any use case high-risk under the EU AI Act?","Who is accountable per agent, and what does within the hour mean in an incident?"],"options":[{"name":"Managed path: prompt agents where configuration is enough, hosted agent for the crew, one registry","when":"Most agents are configuration or code that can live in Foundry, and the GA bar tolerates a dated waiver for hosting.","tradeoff":"Cost follows active sessions, hosted agents had no GA row on 2026-10-13 and you own checkpoint storage for long waits."},{"name":"Self-host the crew on AKS or Container Apps and register it","when":"Code or data must stay off Azure, or the platform team already runs the cluster.","tradeoff":"You run isolation, scaling, identity wiring and session persistence, and governance reaches the code only through the SDK or registry sync."},{"name":"Rebuild everything in Copilot Studio","when":"Makers rather than engineers own the agents and low-code reach is enough.","tradeoff":"Less room for custom orchestration and checkpointed approvals, and some studio features were preview on 2026-10-13."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would ask who each agent acts for, how reversible and external its actions are, whether approvals wait for days, where code must run, which previews are allowed and who is accountable. Those answers set runtime, identity mode and approval tiers before I name a product."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** The managed path uses prompt agents where configuration is enough and a hosted agent for the crew. Self-hosting on AKS or Container Apps is for code that must stay elsewhere. Copilot Studio fits maker-owned agents. All three join one governance layer, the Agent 365 registry, so governance does not choose the runtime."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick and flip.** The crew goes on a hosted agent exposing Responses, so Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot publishing works, with a dated waiver for hosting status. The approval-gated process is a workflow: it raises a human request, checkpoints to storage I control and resumes with the response, so a multi-day wait costs nothing and survives restarts. Hosted recovery alone is not enough, because it re-enters the handler from the beginning and is not deterministic replay. Actions are tiered: reads run free, reversible internal writes are sampled, external or irreversible actions need one approval, regulated ones two reviewers, all failing closed with binding on. Identity is OBO for assistive agents, S2S under a separate blueprint for scheduled jobs and Agentic-User only if the Frontier preview is acceptable. Every identity has a sponsor, Conditional Access replaces MFA, and the partner agent is registered as external with the narrowest scopes it needs."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Flip to self-hosting when code or data must stay off Azure. Then I supply session persistence and register through the SDK or registry sync.","Flip to Copilot Studio when makers own the agent and low-code reach is enough.","Flip to sampled review when actions are reversible and internal, and to two reviewers when the system is high-risk under the EU AI Act.","Flip to the Durable Extension when waits must use no compute, once Beta and preview status is acceptable."]},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["Approvals become rubber stamps","Catch rate on seeded bad requests, median review time, approval rate near 100 percent"],["A fabricated or replayed approval runs a privileged call","Alert on approvals with no matching pending request, and keep response binding on"],["A restart loses a long wait","Kill a worker mid-wait in a drill and confirm the request returns from checkpoints"],["Agents missing from the registry","Registry summary of agents without owners and unmanaged agents, against the deployment inventory"],["The kill switch fails in a real incident","Quarterly drill: the sponsor disables an agent identity and we time how long calls keep working"],["A preview dependency is retired","Dated register: Workflows 2026-12-01, classic agents 2027-03-31"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I would track registry coverage, agents without a sponsor (target zero), time to disable in drills, approval catch rate and review time, task success on a golden set, 403 rate and cost per active session. Rollout starts with an Individual-scope pilot, then Organization scope after admin approval. A canary is a second agent, because a hosted endpoint serves one version. On regulation, Article 50 transparency rules apply from 2026-08-02, so the agent discloses that it is AI, and the Omnibus moved Annex III high-risk rules to 2027-12-02, which sets the logging and review depth for any high-risk use case."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about user presence, action risk, code location, preview tolerance and accountability before naming a product","w":3},{"pt":"Compares at least two runtimes and says where each loses","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses an identity mode per agent type and explains the token subject, sponsor and owner roles","w":3},{"pt":"Designs tiered approval gates that are bound to the request and fail closed","w":3},{"pt":"Handles a multi-day wait with checkpoints and does not trust restart recovery alone","w":2},{"pt":"Governs the whole fleet: registry, partner agent, kill switch drill, retirement","w":2},{"pt":"Date-stamps previews, retirements and regulation","w":2},{"pt":"Names detection signals for each failure mode, metrics and a staged rollout with a second-agent canary","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"The partner agent runs on another cloud and you cannot instrument it. What do you do?","a":"Register it as an external agent, give it the narrowest identity and scopes you control, and treat its outputs as untrusted input. Put a gateway in front of what it can call so you can log and block there."},{"q":"Approval rate is 99.8 percent and median review time is four seconds. What is wrong?","a":"That pattern is rubber-stamping. Show reviewers the arguments and sources, seed known-bad requests and measure the catch rate, and tier actions so low-risk calls never reach the queue."},{"q":"The sponsor of an agent leaves the company. How do you avoid an orphan?","a":"Every identity and blueprint needs at least one sponsor, so reassign before offboarding. The registry summary shows agents without owners, which makes the gap a weekly check."}],"redFlags":["One blueprint or one shared secret for every agent","Approval attached to a single tool, with no tiering and no fail-closed default","Treats a GA product announcement as proof that every mode, including hosted and Agentic-User, is GA","No named sponsor, so nobody can answer for an agent","Has a kill switch on a slide but never times it"]},"refs":["r49","r50","r51","r53","r54","r55","r59","r79","r80","r81","r82","r83","r84"],"takeaways":["Ask who the agent acts for, how reversible its actions are and where code must run before naming a runtime","Tier approvals by action class, bind them to the request and fail closed","Long waits belong in checkpoints you control, not in a process or a restart-recovery promise","Every agent needs a sponsor, its own identity and a kill switch you have timed, and every preview you rely on needs a date"]},{"id":"w19-l5","order":5,"title":"Trust under pressure: guardrails, a leak, evaluation and observability","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"A regulated assistant is live with guardrails, an evaluation gate and tracing, and a user reports a snippet from a document they cannot open. Contain it, find the path, prove the fix and keep your traces from becoming a second leak.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The setup: someone else's snippet on your screen","level":2,"id":"the-setup-someone-else-s-snippet-on-your-screen"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's setup","known":["A regulated RAG assistant is in production with input and output guards, an evaluation gate and tracing","A user reports an answer that quoted a document they are not allowed to open","Security wants the cause, legal wants the clock, and nobody wants the investigation to create a new leak"],"ask":["Which permission model sits behind retrieval, and how does the index learn about changes?","Is there a cache in front of retrieval, and how is it keyed?","Could the snippet have come from a link or image the answer rendered?","What do traces hold today, and who can read them?","Is this a high-risk system, and when does reporting start?"],"tension":"Containment pulls toward switching things off at once. Evidence pulls toward capturing everything. Privacy pulls toward capturing nothing."},{"t":"h","text":"Where the leak can hide","level":2,"id":"where-the-leak-can-hide"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w19-l5-leak-paths","title":"Four places a forbidden snippet can enter an answer","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User question] --> C{Cache hit?}\n  C -->|hit| R[Answer served]\n  C -->|miss| F[ACL filter on index]\n  F --> M[Model with chunks]\n  M --> G[Output guard]\n  G --> R\n  M -.-> T[Trace spans]\n  F -.-> L[Retrieval log]","alt":"A question reaches a cache. A hit serves the answer directly. A miss goes through the ACL filter, the model and the output guard before serving. The model and the filter also write to trace spans and the retrieval log.","caption":"Stale ACLs sit at the filter, a shared cache skips it, injected text exits through the output, and the dotted paths are where an investigation can leak content again."},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials across four chapters","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials-across-four-chapters"},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l5-first-control","title":"Contain the leak: which control first?","prompt":"Flip the leak path, the revocation speed and what your traces hold. The ranking shows the control to ship first.","dials":[{"id":"path","label":"Leak path","values":[{"id":"stale","label":"Stale or wrong ACL in the index"},{"id":"cache","label":"A cache served another user's answer"},{"id":"exfil","label":"Injected text carried data out through rendered output"}]},{"id":"fresh","label":"Revocation must take effect","values":[{"id":"lag","label":"After the next sync, hours are fine"},{"id":"immediate","label":"Immediately"}]},{"id":"traces","label":"What traces hold","values":[{"id":"refs","label":"Content stored outside, referenced from spans"},{"id":"none","label":"No content recorded"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prefilter","name":"Fix the ACL filter and re-sync permissions","summary":"Security filters on the index, fail closed, rebuild stale ACL fields."},{"id":"live","name":"Live permission check at read time","summary":"Ask the source system before a chunk reaches the model."},{"id":"cachescope","name":"Scope every cache by tenant and user","summary":"Partition keys so one user's entry is never another's hit."},{"id":"outguard","name":"Constrain the output","summary":"Allow-list links and images, block data-carrying markdown, check groundedness."},{"id":"replay","name":"Replay from traces and add a CI test","summary":"Turn the incident into a permanent regression case."}],"scores":{"prefilter":{"path":{"stale":{"s":2,"why":"Fixes the filter or stale ACL fields that let the document through, then re-syncs."},"cache":{"s":-2,"why":"A cache hit never reaches retrieval, so index filters cannot help."},"exfil":{"s":-1,"why":"Access control was correct, so the filter is not what failed."}},"fresh":{"lag":{"s":1,"why":"Scheduled sync is enough when hours of lag are acceptable."},"immediate":{"s":-2,"why":"Index copies lag: indexers run every 5 minutes at best and notifications can take up to 6 hours."}},"traces":{"refs":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."}}},"live":{"path":{"stale":{"s":2,"why":"Re-checking the source at read time ignores the stale copy."},"cache":{"s":-2,"why":"A cached answer skips the live check as well."},"exfil":{"s":-1,"why":"Access was granted correctly, so a fresh check changes nothing."}},"fresh":{"lag":{"s":-1,"why":"Adds a source call per candidate for lag the dial allows."},"immediate":{"s":2,"why":"Revocation takes effect on the next read."}},"traces":{"refs":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."}}},"cachescope":{"path":{"stale":{"s":-1,"why":"Does not touch an index that holds wrong ACLs."},"cache":{"s":2,"why":"Partition keys by tenant and user, for example vary-by on the API Management semantic cache."},"exfil":{"s":-1,"why":"The leak is in the output, not the cache."}},"fresh":{"lag":{"s":0,"why":"Fine while entries expire within the lag the dial allows."},"immediate":{"s":-1,"why":"A scoped entry still outlives a revocation until it expires or is purged."}},"traces":{"refs":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."}}},"outguard":{"path":{"stale":{"s":-1,"why":"Groundedness checks do not know permissions, so a grounded answer from a forbidden document passes."},"cache":{"s":-1,"why":"The cached answer is grounded, so the check passes it."},"exfil":{"s":2,"why":"Allow-list links and images and block markdown that carries data out."}},"fresh":{"lag":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of revocation speed."},"immediate":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of revocation speed."}},"traces":{"refs":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."},"none":{"s":0,"why":"Does not depend on trace content."}}},"replay":{"path":{"stale":{"s":1,"why":"Add a revoked-user case to the gate."},"cache":{"s":1,"why":"Add a two-user cache case to the gate."},"exfil":{"s":1,"why":"Add an injected-document case to the gate."}},"fresh":{"lag":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of revocation speed."},"immediate":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of revocation speed."}},"traces":{"refs":{"s":1,"why":"References on spans let you replay the failing request against ACL-protected content."},"none":{"s":-1,"why":"Nothing to replay from, so you rebuild the case from the report."}}}},"takeaway":"Contain on the path that failed, then turn it into a regression test. A guard never substitutes for the permission check, and a trace is only as safe as what you let it hold."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Containment first, evidence second, a named leak path third. Then the proof: seeded users, repeated trials and a gate. Say where the output guard stops being useful and why traces default to no content."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Some users in a very large number of groups see every document, and nobody else does. What is wrong with this code?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"groups = token.get(\"groups\", [])\nflt = None\nif groups:\n    ids = \",\".join(groups)\n    flt = f\"group_ids/any(g: search.in(g, '{ids}'))\"\nresults = client.search(query, filter=flt)"},"options":["The search call must use the admin key, otherwise the service ignores the filter expression on every request","A user in too many groups gets an overage indicator, not a groups claim, so the empty list drops the filter","Group ids cannot be stored in a collection field, so the filter must be built from user principal names instead","The filter must go in a request header, because a filter in the body is applied only to the first page of results"],"answer":1,"explain":"A JWT carries at most 200 groups. Beyond that an overage indicator replaces the groups claim, so `token.get(\"groups\", [])` returns an empty list, `if groups` skips the filter and the query runs unfiltered: a fail-open bug. Detect overage, resolve membership through Microsoft Graph (transitive membership pages hold 100 by default and 999 at most) and fail closed, for example public documents only, when groups cannot be resolved. Nothing in the snippet depends on key type or filter syntax, so the other options describe problems that are not in the code.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l5-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each guard to what it checks.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt Shields user prompt attack","r":"The user's message, for rule-changing, role-play and encoding attacks"},{"l":"Prompt Shields document attack","r":"Retrieved or uploaded content that carries hidden instructions"},{"l":"Groundedness detection","r":"The answer text, compared against its grounding sources"},{"l":"Protected material detection","r":"Completions that reproduce known text or code"},{"l":"Spotlighting","r":"Marking documents so the model can tell data from instructions"}],"explain":"Each guard watches a different edge. Prompt Shields has two shields, one for the user's message and one for documents (the standalone API takes one prompt and up to five documents). Groundedness compares the answer with its sources and says nothing about permissions. Protected material scans completions. Spotlighting tags documents, for example by base64 encoding, so the model treats them as data, at the cost of more tokens.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l5-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A permission test passes on 80 percent of single trials. The suite repeats each test 5 times and the gate needs all 5 to pass, with independent trials. What pass^5 should you expect, in percent?","answer":32.8,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"%","hint":"pass^k is the chance that all k trials succeed.","explain":"pass^k for a per-trial success p is p^k, so 0.8^5 = 0.32768, or about 32.8 percent. A test that looks 80 percent reliable fails a five-trial gate two times in three. That is why non-deterministic checks need a repeat count, a tolerance policy decided in advance and a fix for flaky tests rather than a lower bar.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l5-q4","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Content capture is off in both settings below, yet customer text appears in traces. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"# environment\n# ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA=false\n# OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=false\n\n@trace_function\ndef lookup_customer(email: str, notes: str) -> dict:\n    ..."},"options":["The `@trace_function` decorator records parameters and return values whatever the content flag says","Sampling at five traces per second keeps content-bearing spans even when the content flag is turned off","Python instrumentation has been on by default since 1.6.0, and default-on instrumentation also turns content capture on","Cached tokens are copied into span attributes, so the content flag cannot hide them from the trace"],"answer":0,"explain":"The decorator always records parameters and return values, so `email` and `notes` land on the span whatever the flag says. Fix it by removing the decorator on tools that handle personal data, redacting at the exporter or wrapping with your own span. Sampling decides which traces are kept, not what they hold. Default-on instrumentation adds spans but not content, and cached tokens appear as counts only.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l5-q5","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the response to the reported leak.","items":["Contain: switch off the suspect path within the first hour","Preserve evidence through trace and retrieval-log references without widening content capture","Expand the investigation over 24 hours: other users, tenants and paths","Fix at the source over days or weeks: filter, cache key or output control","Add the case to the CI gate and re-run the permission suite"],"explain":"Containment comes first because the leak is live. Evidence is preserved before anything is changed, and scope expands once the first path is closed. The fix belongs at the source, and the regression test makes sure the fix stays. Microsoft's incident guidance describes containment in the first hour, expansion in the first 24 hours and a fix at source over days or weeks.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l5-q6","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A pairwise judge prefers whichever answer is shown first. You can afford one extra judge pass per item. Which spend reduces the bias most?","options":["Re-run the same order a second time at temperature 0 and average the two scores together","Move to a larger judge model and keep a single pass in the original answer order","Add a longer rubric to the judge prompt and keep a single pass in the original order","Run each pair in both orders and count a win only when both verdicts agree"],"answer":3,"explain":"Swapping the order cancels position bias, and disagreement flags unreliable items. In MT-Bench, consistency after swapping was 65.0 percent for GPT-4, 46.2 for GPT-3.5 and 23.8 for Claude-v1 [[r73]]. A separate study of order bias found that reordering let Vicuna-13B beat ChatGPT on 66 of 80 queries when ChatGPT judged. Repeating the same order repeats the bias. A larger judge helps but does not remove it. A rubric guides criteria, not position. The cost is double judge spend and more ties.","difficulty":2}],"defend":{"prompt":"A user of our regulated RAG assistant says an answer quoted a document they are not allowed to open. We have guardrails, an evaluation gate and tracing. Walk me through the first hour, how you find the cause, how you prove the fix and how you keep traces from becoming a second leak.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Which permission model sits behind retrieval, and how does the index learn about changes?","Is there a cache in front of retrieval, and how is it keyed?","Could the snippet have come through a rendered link or image rather than retrieval?","How fast must a revoked permission take effect?","What do traces and retrieval logs hold today, and who can read them?","Is this a high-risk system under the EU AI Act, and what does legal need and by when?"],"options":[{"name":"Contain, trace, fix at the source, then gate","when":"The path is unknown and could be a stale ACL, a cache or injected output.","tradeoff":"Slower than a quick patch, and containment may switch a feature off for a day."},{"name":"Add an output guard and move on","when":"The cause is exfiltration through rendered output and access control was correct.","tradeoff":"Groundedness and PII checks do not know permissions, so a grounded answer from a forbidden document passes."},{"name":"Move to per-tenant or per-user indexes","when":"The permission model is simple and stable and a cross-user leak would be severe.","tradeoff":"Index count limits, sync cost and slower updates, and it fixes neither a shared cache nor the output path."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would ask how permissions reach the index, whether a cache sits in front of retrieval, whether the snippet could have left through a link or image, how fast revocation must be, what traces hold and whether a reporting clock applies. The answers rank the suspects."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** Contain, trace and fix at the source is the boring option and the right default. An output guard alone is tempting but does not decide who may see a document. Per-tenant or per-user indexes fit simple, stable permission models and leave cache and output paths open."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick and flip.** In the first hour I switch off the suspect path behind a flag, usually the cache or one connector, keep the assistant up for others and pull the retrieval log and trace ids for the request. Then I test four suspects in order of evidence: a stale ACL (compare the source ACL with the indexed copy), a groups overage that dropped the filter (a user in more than 200 groups loses the groups claim, and fail-open code returns everything), a cache hit served across users, and injected text leaving through a link or image. The fix is at retrieval: security filters on synced ACL fields, fail closed on overage with Graph resolution, tenant and user in every cache key and an allow-list for rendered links. Guards stay as defense in depth, because a groundedness check passes an answer drawn from a forbidden document."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Flip to a live permission check when revocation must be immediate, because indexers run every 5 minutes at best and notifications can take up to 6 hours.","Flip to per-tenant indexes when tenants are few and a leak across them is unacceptable.","Flip the trace policy to external content with references on spans only for a narrow, approved cohort and a short retention."]},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["A revoked user still retrieves the document","Timed revocation drill: remove a test user, query repeatedly, record minutes until denied"],["Groups overage drops the filter","CI user with 250 groups, log overage events, alert on any query run with no filter"],["A cache answers across users","Two-user test on the same question, cache logs keyed by user and tenant"],["Injected text carries data out through a link or image","Seeded poisoned document in the suite, alert on rendered URLs outside the allow-list"],["Traces hold customer text","Weekly audit for spans carrying input or output messages, including decorated tool functions"],["The gate passes one lucky run","Repeat each permission test k times and require all to pass"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** The hard gate is zero cross-user leaks on a versioned permission suite with a revoked user, a 250-group user, a cache pair and an injected document, run as repeated trials. I also track p95 time to revoke, overage fail-closed rate, guard block and false-positive rates, and the count of spans that carry content. The fix ships behind a flag, shadow-compares returned document ids against the old filter, then canaries before it widens. Legal gets the facts early: if the system is high-risk, Article 73 timers run from awareness, and Annex III obligations apply from 2027-12-02 under the Omnibus, so counsel confirms which regime applies."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about permission model, caches, evidence, revocation speed, trace policy and the legal clock","w":3},{"pt":"Contains first and preserves evidence before changing anything","w":2},{"pt":"Lists the leak paths and ties each to a detection step","w":3},{"pt":"Fixes at retrieval (pre-filter, fail closed on overage, optional live check) and explains why guards cannot substitute","w":3},{"pt":"Proves the fix with seeded users, repeated trials and a CI gate","w":3},{"pt":"Keeps traces safe: content off by default or stored outside with references, retention and access","w":2},{"pt":"Gives metrics and a staged rollout with shadow comparison","w":2},{"pt":"Names flips such as live check versus pre-filter by revocation speed","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"The postmortem says the groundedness check passed the leaking answer. Is that a guard bug?","a":"No. Groundedness compares an answer with its sources, and the forbidden document was a valid source. The control that decides which documents may be a source is the permission filter, so the fix belongs in retrieval."},{"q":"Engineers want full prompt and answer capture on every span to investigate. Do you agree?","a":"Not globally. The convention says instrumentations should not capture content by default and recommends external storage with references on spans for production. Use a narrow, time-boxed capture for the affected cohort with a named approver, short retention and the same ACLs as the source."},{"q":"An auditor asks you to prove a revoked user cannot read the document. What do you show?","a":"The permission suite: seeded revoked and many-group users, repeated trials in CI with zero tolerated leaks, the timed revocation drill and immutable retrieval logs."}],"redFlags":["Answers with a stronger system prompt that says never reveal other users' documents","Treats a groundedness or PII guard as the access control","Switches on full content capture everywhere to investigate","Calls it fixed after one manual retest with no revoked or many-group user","Has no containment step, so the leak keeps running while the team debugs"]},"refs":["r1","r10","r11","r14","r15","r16","r18","r23","r56","r58","r73","r74","r75","r78","r85","r86"],"takeaways":["Contain on the path that failed, then make it a regression test","Guards check content, not permissions: access control lives in retrieval and fails closed","A groups overage or a shared cache can bypass a correct filter, so test both with seeded users","Non-deterministic gates need repeated trials, and pairwise judges need both orders","Traces default to no content, and a decorator can override that default"]},{"id":"w19-l6","order":6,"title":"Beyond text: multimodal, reasoning and open-model choices","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer wants an assistant that sees photos, reads visually rich pages and talks, and the team is split between a hosted reasoning model and an open model it serves itself. Leave the dials open, then decide the modality path, the model home and the safety stance.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The setup: eyes, ears and a model choice","level":2,"id":"the-setup-eyes-ears-and-a-model-choice"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's setup","known":["People away from a desk photograph equipment and pages, then ask questions out loud","The pages carry tables, diagrams and small print, and the network is unreliable","One camp wants a hosted frontier reasoning model, the other wants an open model served in house"],"ask":["How fast must a voice turn feel, and what happens when the network drops?","How complex is the page layout, how many pages, and how often do they change?","May audio and images leave the site, and which residency or key rules apply?","Are the questions lookups, or multi-step diagnosis that needs reasoning?","Which languages are in play, and can a photo carry text an attacker controls?"],"tension":"Voice wants speed and a short path. Documents want layout fidelity. Reasoning wants time. Open models want steady load and an operations team."},{"t":"h","text":"One question, three decisions","level":2,"id":"one-question-three-decisions"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w19-l6-turn-path","title":"What happens to one photo-and-voice turn","src":"flowchart LR\n  P[Photo or page image] --> S[Size and detail choice]\n  V[Voice turn] --> T[Turn detection]\n  S --> R[Retrieve pages and text]\n  T --> R\n  R --> M{Router}\n  M -->|lookup| F[Fast model]\n  M -->|hard| H[Reasoning model]\n  F --> A[Answer and voice out]\n  H --> A","alt":"A photo goes through a size and detail choice and a voice turn goes through turn detection. Both feed retrieval. A router then sends lookups to a fast model and hard questions to a reasoning model, and both produce the answer and voice output.","caption":"Chapter 13 owns the left half, chapter 18 the router and the reasoning branch, chapter 1 the budgets and parameters that make both branches work."},{"t":"h","text":"Numbers worth carrying into the room","level":2,"id":"numbers-worth-carrying-into-the-room"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"1,229 tokens","value":"1024 x 1024 at high","note":"Patch models: ceil(w/32) x ceil(h/32) patches, times 1.2 on gpt-5.x"},{"label":"1 token per 100 ms","value":"User audio in realtime","note":"1 per 50 ms of assistant audio, and the conversation is resent every turn"},{"label":"257.5 KB","value":"Per page for ColPali","note":"Against 8.6 KB per page for BGE-M3"},{"label":"25,000 tokens","value":"Reserve for reasoning and output","note":"OpenAI and Azure advice when starting with a reasoning model"}]},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials across three chapters","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials-across-three-chapters"},{"t":"dials","id":"w19-l6-turn-path-board","title":"Pick the path for a turn","prompt":"Flip turn latency, the data boundary and how hard the questions are. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"turn","label":"Turn latency target","values":[{"id":"conv","label":"Conversational, about a second"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Several seconds are fine"}]},{"id":"data","label":"Data boundary","values":[{"id":"cloud","label":"Cloud processing is allowed"},{"id":"site","label":"Audio and images must stay on site"}]},{"id":"think","label":"Question difficulty","values":[{"id":"lookup","label":"Mostly lookups and extraction"},{"id":"hard","label":"Hard multi-step diagnosis"}]}],"options":[{"id":"s2s","name":"Hosted speech-to-speech","summary":"One realtime model hears and speaks, with a retrieval tool for facts."},{"id":"cascade","name":"Hosted cascaded pipeline","summary":"Speech to text, a routed text model, then text to speech."},{"id":"self","name":"Self-served open model","summary":"vLLM or KAITO on GPUs you run, behind the same cascade."},{"id":"edge","name":"On-device model with cloud fallback","summary":"A device runtime such as Foundry Local for the first hop."},{"id":"reason","name":"Reasoning route with an interim reply","summary":"Hard turns go to a reasoning model while a short reply keeps the user informed."}],"scores":{"s2s":{"turn":{"conv":{"s":2,"why":"Audio in and out over WebRTC, about 100 ms in Azure docs, with no text hops."},"relaxed":{"s":-1,"why":"Audio tokens bill each turn and the conversation is resent, so you pay for speed you do not need."}},"data":{"cloud":{"s":1,"why":"Hosted audio models need no GPUs of yours."},"site":{"s":-2,"why":"Audio leaves the site."}},"think":{"lookup":{"s":1,"why":"Lookups through a retrieval tool fit."},"hard":{"s":-1,"why":"Reasoning usually has to be delegated to another model, which adds a hop."}}},"cascade":{"turn":{"conv":{"s":-1,"why":"Three hops add latency before the first audio."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Text between stages carries guardrails, tools and routing."}},"data":{"cloud":{"s":1,"why":"All hosted, nothing to run."},"site":{"s":-2,"why":"Audio and images still leave the site."}},"think":{"lookup":{"s":1,"why":"A small text model with retrieval is enough."},"hard":{"s":1,"why":"The text stage is the natural place to route to a reasoning model."}}},"self":{"turn":{"conv":{"s":0,"why":"Fast on good GPUs, but you own queueing and warm capacity."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Throughput engines such as vLLM suit steady, batch-friendly load."}},"data":{"cloud":{"s":0,"why":"You run GPUs and patching for no data reason."},"site":{"s":2,"why":"Weights run where the data is allowed to stay."}},"think":{"lookup":{"s":1,"why":"Open models handle extraction and lookup well enough to test."},"hard":{"s":-1,"why":"Smaller open models can lose ground on multi-step diagnosis, so measure first."}}},"edge":{"turn":{"conv":{"s":1,"why":"No network hop for the first stage."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Latency is not the constraint."}},"data":{"cloud":{"s":-1,"why":"Device-size models limit quality for no gain."},"site":{"s":2,"why":"Audio and images never leave the device."}},"think":{"lookup":{"s":1,"why":"Small models handle extraction and lookup."},"hard":{"s":-2,"why":"A single-user device runtime with device-size models cannot carry hard diagnosis."}}},"reason":{"turn":{"conv":{"s":-2,"why":"Reasoning effort adds seconds to every turn."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Spend time where the task pays for it."}},"data":{"cloud":{"s":1,"why":"Hosted reasoning models need no GPUs of yours."},"site":{"s":-2,"why":"A hosted model sees the data."}},"think":{"lookup":{"s":-1,"why":"Reasoning tokens bill as output and are wasted on lookups."},"hard":{"s":2,"why":"Test-time compute helps multi-step diagnosis."}}}},"takeaway":"Route by turn, not by system: a fast path for lookups, a reasoning path for hard questions, and a data boundary that decides who may host either."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"A number for voice latency, a reason for the voice path, token arithmetic for images, a measured reason to run your own GPUs and a stance on text hidden in photos."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l6-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A request carries 6 photos of 1600 x 900 pixels at high detail. The model uses 32 px patches, a 2,500-patch budget at high and a 1.2 multiplier, rounded up. How many image tokens does the request use?","answer":10440,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Patches per photo are ceil(w/32) x ceil(h/32).","explain":"Each photo is ceil(1600/32) = 50 patches wide and ceil(900/32) = 29 tall, so 1,450 patches, which is under the 2,500 budget and is not resized. 1,450 x 1.2 = 1,740 tokens per photo, and 6 photos give 10,440. Measure real usage per model: on the gpt-5.4 family `low` uses a 2048 px and 6,144-patch budget and can cost more than `high`.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Per-turn input tokens keep growing in a long realtime voice session, as the usage log shows. Why, and what is the fix?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"turn  input_tokens  cached_tokens\n   1        1,150              0\n   4        5,900          3,200\n   8       12,700          9,600\n  12       19,300         14,100"},"options":["User audio is billed at 1 token per 10 ms, so input scales with speaking time only and cannot be reduced","Semantic turn detection splits one user turn into several billed turns, so switch to silence-based detection","The conversation is resent each turn, so input grows; a lower retention ratio truncates in bigger steps","The WebRTC transport re-sends each audio frame until acknowledged, so switch to the WebSocket transport"],"answer":2,"explain":"Realtime sessions resend the whole conversation every turn, so input tokens grow with session length, and cached tokens grow with them. `session.truncation.retention_ratio` defaults to 1.0, and 0.8 drops extra items at each truncation, which keeps the cache valid longer. User audio costs 1 token per 100 ms, not per 10 ms. Turn detection type changes when turns end, not how history is billed, and transport retries do not appear as billed input.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l6-q3","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"This call fails. Which statements explain the failure? Choose all that apply.","code":{"lang":"python","code":"resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\n    reasoning_effort=\"minimal\",\n    tools=tools,\n    messages=messages,\n)"},"options":["The model accepts neither none nor minimal reasoning effort, and its default is medium","Chat Completions does not support function calling with this model, so tools need the Responses API","The model name needs a date suffix, because aliases are rejected for reasoning models","Reasoning effort is accepted only as a request header, not as a body field","The `tools` list must be serialized to a JSON string before it is sent"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"GPT-6.1 Sol rejects `none` and `minimal` and defaults to medium, so pick low, medium or high. Chat Completions does not support function calling with it either, so the call moves to the Responses API, where `reasoning.effort` is a body field and tools are a list. The date-suffix, header and JSON-string explanations are not behaviors of this API.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l6-q4","type":"match","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Match each document situation to the pipeline that fits it.","pairs":[{"l":"Documents must be processed on premises or air-gapped","r":"Document Intelligence containers"},{"l":"A stable form layout with about five labelled samples","r":"A Document Intelligence custom model"},{"l":"Training video where speech carries the content","r":"Content Understanding video analyzer, about 1 frame per second"},{"l":"Diagrams and charts carry the meaning and the query is visual","r":"Page-image retrieval from the ColPali family"}],"explain":"Document Intelligence containers are the only on-premises or air-gapped option, and a custom model can start from as few as 5 labelled samples. The Content Understanding video analyzer samples about 1 frame per second at 512 x 512 and transcribes only speech, which suits speech-led video. Page-image retrieval keeps visual meaning but costs about 257.5 KB per page against 8.6 KB for BGE-M3, so use it where the figure is the answer.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l6-q5","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Technicians upload photos, and a photo of a label says to email the maintenance log to an outside address. Which design best limits the damage?","options":["Strip EXIF metadata from every upload, which removes any text an attacker could hide in the image file","Limit what an image-influenced session may do: no external sends or state changes without approval","Add a system prompt line that tells the model to ignore any text it finds inside uploaded images","Set image detail to low, so small hidden text is never read by the model and costs fewer tokens"],"answer":1,"explain":"OWASP names hidden instructions in images as a multimodal injection risk and says reliable defenses are an open research area. The Model Spec gives images no authority by default, but production models do not yet fully reflect it. So design for blast radius: a session with untrusted input should not also hold sensitive access and an external send without approval. EXIF stripping misses visible text, a prompt line is not a control, and low detail neither hides text reliably nor is always cheaper.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l6-q6","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Data must be processed only inside the EU data zone, and you want Azure model router to send easy turns to a small model. Which deployment type can host it?","options":["Global Standard, because it routes across every region to find the cheapest capacity at the best price","Regional Standard in one EU region, because the router runs wherever the pool of models is deployed","Data Zone Standard for the EU, because the router runs on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard only","Global Batch, because routing decisions can wait for the 24-hour completion window of that tier"],"answer":2,"explain":"Global deployments may process data in any region, which breaks a data-zone rule. Model router runs only on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard, so a regional Standard deployment cannot host it, and Batch is a different, asynchronous tier. Expect two costs: the router's effective context window is the smallest model in its pool, and caching helps only when consecutive requests reach the same model.","difficulty":3}],"defend":{"prompt":"We want a voice-and-camera assistant for people working away from a desk. They photograph equipment and visually rich pages, ask questions out loud and have an unreliable network. The team is split between a hosted frontier reasoning model and an open model we serve ourselves. Design the modality and model strategy.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["How fast must a voice turn feel, and what should happen when the network drops?","How complex are the pages, how many are there, and how often do they change?","May audio and images leave the site, and which residency or key rules apply?","Are the questions lookups or multi-step diagnosis, and which languages are used?","Is traffic steady or spiky, and does the team already run GPUs?","What can the assistant do, and can a photo carry text an attacker controls?"],"options":[{"name":"Hosted cascaded pipeline with a router","when":"Control, guardrails and tool use matter more than the last few hundred milliseconds, and data may go to a hosted region.","tradeoff":"Three hops add latency, and a reasoning route needs an interim reply."},{"name":"Hosted speech-to-speech with a retrieval tool","when":"A conversational feel is the product and questions are mostly lookups.","tradeoff":"Audio tokens bill every turn with the history resent, and guardrails and routing are harder to place."},{"name":"Self-served open model, with a hosted fallback for hard turns","when":"Data must stay on site, or steady volume keeps GPUs busy and you can run an operations team.","tradeoff":"You own GPUs, patching and a re-evaluation on every weights or serving change."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would ask for the voice latency target, page complexity and volume, the data boundary, how hard the questions are, traffic shape and what a hidden instruction in a photo could trigger. Those answers set the voice path, the document pipeline and the model home."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** A hosted cascaded pipeline is the boring default. Speech-to-speech wins on feel and loses on control and cost growth. A self-served open model wins on boundary and steady load and loses on operations and hard-task quality."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick and flip.** I would start with the cascade: the text between speech stages is where guardrails, tools and a router live. Lookups go to a fast model, and hard diagnosis goes to a reasoning model with an interim reply, because reasoning tokens bill as output, share the window and need at least 25,000 tokens of headroom at first. Pages go through layout extraction with figures verbalized, and page-image retrieval is added only where the figure is the answer, since ColPali costs about 257.5 KB per page against 8.6 KB for BGE-M3. Photos are resized by patch arithmetic and measured per model, because low detail is not always cheaper. A short manual set could go whole into the prompt, but long-context accuracy drops well before the advertised window, so I keep retrieval. Anything a photo says is data, and an image-influenced session cannot send externally or change state without approval."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Flip to speech-to-speech when sub-second feel is the product and questions are lookups. Then I cap history with a retention ratio of 0.8 and tune turn detection.","Flip to self-serving when audio or images must stay on site, or when steady load keeps GPUs busy. vLLM reports 2 to 4 times the throughput of earlier systems, and Foundry Local covers a single-user device while multi-user serving points to vLLM or Triton.","Flip back to hosted data-zone deployments when GPU utilization is low, because residency, zero data retention, customer-managed keys and private endpoints already cover many boundaries."]},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["The voice bill grows with session length","Per-turn input and cached tokens against turn number, and the retention ratio in effect"],["The assistant cuts users off or lags in noisy places","Barge-in and cut-off rates by environment, turn detection settings under noise"],["A figure is misread from a photo (small text, rotation)","Page-level retrieval hit and answer accuracy on a labelled photo set"],["Hidden text in a photo steers a tool call","Injected-image cases in the red-team suite, alerts on tool calls that follow image input"],["A reasoning route returns empty or incomplete","Status incomplete at max_output_tokens before visible output, headroom below 25,000 tokens"],["Open-model quality regresses after a change","Golden-set gate per weights and serving version, pinned versions"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I would track time to first audio at p95, task success on a golden set that includes photo and noisy-voice cases, page-level retrieval hit rate, cost per resolved task by route, the share of turns sent to the reasoning route, tokens per language (the same text can cost up to 15 times more across languages with 2023 tokenizers) and GPU utilization if we self-serve. Rollout shadows a sample of turns to the alternative path, canaries by route and keeps a fallback when the preferred model or network fails."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about voice latency, page complexity, data boundary, question difficulty, traffic shape and risk","w":3},{"pt":"Chooses a voice path with a stated reason and explains how each path's cost and control behave","w":3},{"pt":"Picks the document pipeline by layout complexity and says when page-image retrieval pays","w":2},{"pt":"Budgets image tokens with the patch arithmetic and knows low detail is not always cheaper","w":2},{"pt":"Routes lookups and hard turns, reserves reasoning headroom and uses an interim reply","w":2},{"pt":"Compares hosted and self-served models on control, cost and compliance with flip conditions","w":3},{"pt":"Treats text in images as untrusted and limits the blast radius of image-influenced sessions","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure signals, metrics and a shadow and canary rollout","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"Finance says self-serving looks cheaper per token. What do you check?","a":"Utilization, not list price. Steady traffic keeps GPUs busy and spiky traffic does not. Add operations, patching and a re-evaluation on each weights or serving change, then compare with batch, caching and data-zone pricing on your own prompts."},{"q":"A handwritten note in a photo tells the assistant to email the maintenance log. What happens?","a":"Image text has no authority, but no defense is reliable, so the design caps the damage. A session with untrusted input must not also hold sensitive access and an external send without approval. Log the attempted tool call and test this case in the red-team suite."},{"q":"Voice is fast in the lab and slow on site. What do you test?","a":"The network path (Azure lists about 100 ms for WebRTC and about 200 ms for WebSocket), turn detection under noise, region distance and whether the reasoning route fires without an interim reply."}],"redFlags":["Chooses speech-to-speech or an open model because it sounds modern, with no latency, data or utilization number","Assumes low image detail is always cheaper and never measures tokens per model","Sends every turn to a reasoning model, including simple lookups","Treats text found inside a photo as instructions the assistant may follow","Compares token prices and ignores GPU utilization and operations cost"]},"refs":["r4","r16","r27","r61","r62","r64","r65","r66","r67","r68","r69","r70","r71"],"takeaways":["Route by turn: a fast path for lookups, a reasoning path for hard questions","A cascade gives control, speech-to-speech gives feel, and the realtime history is resent every turn","Image tokens follow patch arithmetic per model, and low detail is not always cheaper","Run your own GPUs for the data boundary or steady load, not for list price","Text inside an image is data with no authority, so cap what an image-influenced session can do"]},{"id":"w19-l7","order":7,"title":"Rapid fire and the final checklist","kind":"concept","minutes":35,"brief":"Twenty-four questions, one or more per chapter, then the ten questions to ask about any AI system you have never seen and the five-step method that turns the answers into a defended design. Run the quiz against a timer, then keep the table as your map.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"How to run the rapid fire","level":2,"id":"how-to-run-the-rapid-fire"},{"t":"p","md":"Give each question about 90 seconds and say the dial that decides it out loud before you pick. The set covers every chapter from the orientation to the field notes, in all five question types. A miss is a pointer: the explanation names the mechanism, and the table below tells you which topic to reopen."},{"t":"h","text":"Ten questions to ask about any unfamiliar AI system","level":2,"id":"ten-questions-to-ask-about-any-unfamiliar-ai-system"},{"t":"table","caption":"Ask these in order. Each answer lives in the topics listed in the last column.","head":["#","Question","Why it decides the design","Where the answer lives"],"rows":[["1","What is it for, and which actions can it take?","Autonomy and reversibility set the pattern and the oversight level.","`w10-t01`, `w17-t01`, `w17-t02`"],["2","Where does knowledge come from, how does it get in and how does it stay current?","Parsing quality, change detection and deletes cap everything downstream.","`w03-t01`, `w03-t06`, `w03-t09`"],["3","Who may see what, and what enforces it at query time?","The model can only leak what retrieval hands it.","`w07-t06`, `w07-t08`, `w07-t14`, `w07-t15`"],["4","How is content split, embedded and indexed?","Chunk, embedding and filter choices set the recall ceiling.","`w04-t01`, `w04-t12`, `w04-t17`, `w04-t18`"],["5","How does retrieval find the right chunk for exact identifiers and for paraphrases?","Lexical, dense, hybrid and rerank each fail on a different query type.","`w05-t04`, `w05-t05`, `w05-t17`"],["6","Which guards stand where, and what does a false alarm cost?","Guards add latency and refusals, and they check content, not permissions.","`w06-t01`, `w06-t05`, `w06-t10`"],["7","How are state, context and tool access managed?","API choice, token scoping and context budgets decide portability and blast radius.","`w01-t12`, `w08-t11`, `w08-t12`, `w10-t17`"],["8","What does one answer cost, how long does it take and what breaks at a 429 or an outage?","Latency budgets and capacity modes set the user experience under load.","`w02-t17`, `w16-t04`, `w16-t10`"],["9","How do you know it works, and how would you know why it failed?","Evidence before launch and traces after it keep a wrong answer explainable.","`w14-t03`, `w14-t14`, `w15-l1`, `w15-l2`"],["10","Who is accountable, where does it run and which rules apply?","Identity, residency and law decide what may ship at all.","`w12-t15`, `w12-t19`, `w17-t04`, `w18-t06`"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"h","text":"The five-step answer method, recapped","level":2,"id":"the-five-step-answer-method-recapped"},{"t":"flow","title":"Dial and Defend in five minutes","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Clarify the dials","md":"Corpus, update rate, permission model, tenancy, latency, risk and cost.","tag":"1 min"},{"title":"List the options","md":"At least two, including the boring one, each with where it loses.","tag":"1 min"},{"title":"Pick and flip","md":"Choose for the stated dials and name the change that reverses the choice.","tag":"1 min"},{"title":"Name the failures","md":"Symptom, cause and the signal that detects it.","tag":"1 min"},{"title":"Metrics and rollout","md":"Golden set, SLOs, shadow, canary and a kill switch.","tag":"1 min"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"The habit that separates strong answers","md":"Never end on the pick. End on what would change your mind and how you would find out you were wrong. An answer with a flip condition and a detection signal reads as experience, not recall."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w19-l7-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the five steps of the Dial-and-Defend method in order.","items":["Clarify the dials: corpus, update rate, permission model, tenancy, latency, risk and cost","List the options, including the boring one","Pick for the stated dials and say what would flip the choice","Name the failure modes and how you would detect them","Name the metrics and the rollout plan"],"explain":"Clarifying comes first because the dials decide which options are even valid. Options come before the pick so the choice is visible as a trade-off. Failure modes and detection come after the pick, because they belong to the chosen design, and metrics and rollout close the answer with how you would prove it works.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w19-l7-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Replies from the second call ignore the house style set in the system prompt. What is the bug?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"r1 = client.responses.create(model=MODEL, instructions=SYSTEM, input=\"Summarize ticket 41\")\nr2 = client.responses.create(model=MODEL, previous_response_id=r1.id, input=\"Draft a reply\")"},"options":["`instructions` are not carried over by `previous_response_id`, so send them again on the second call","`previous_response_id` works only when `store` is set to false on the first call, so the chain never forms","Chained responses accept only a single hop, so the second call loses everything from the first one","`previous_response_id` cannot be combined with `input`, so the new text overwrites the whole chain"],"answer":0,"explain":"With `previous_response_id`, top-level `instructions` do not carry over, so the second call runs without the system prompt. Responses are stored by default for 30 days, which is what makes chaining work, and the earlier input tokens are billed again on each call. The other options describe limits that do not exist: `store` false would break chaining, chains are not limited to one hop, and `input` is the way to add the new turn.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r25"},{"id":"w19-l7-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"On GPT-5.6 and later models other than GPT-6.1 Sol a cache write costs 1.25 times and a read 0.1 times the uncached input rate. A shared prefix is written once and read 19 more times within the TTL. What is the total prefix cost as a multiple of reading it once at the uncached rate?","answer":3.15,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"x","hint":"One write plus nineteen reads.","explain":"1.25 + 19 x 0.1 = 3.15, against 20 for twenty uncached reads, about 84 percent less. The same arithmetic shows the risk: a prefix written and never reused costs 1.25, a 25 percent premium, so cache only prefixes that repeat within the TTL.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r19"},{"id":"w19-l7-q4","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Users still get answers that cite blobs deleted from storage last week. The blob indexer runs hourly and reports success. What is the cause?","options":["The hourly schedule is below the minimum indexer interval, so every other run is silently skipped by the service","A delete updates LastModified on the blob, so the indexer re-indexes the deleted file with its old content","It tracks changes by LastModified and does not detect deletes without a deletion policy such as soft delete","The semantic ranker keeps the last 50 results cached and serves them even after the source blob is deleted"],"answer":2,"explain":"The blob indexer finds changes through LastModified and does not see deletions unless a deletion detection policy, such as soft delete, is configured. A restored soft-deleted blob is not re-indexed either, because LastModified does not change. The minimum schedule interval is 5 minutes, so hourly is valid, a delete is not a LastModified change, and the semantic ranker works on the live result set rather than a cache.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w19-l7-q5","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An index holds 10 million vectors of 1,536 dimensions stored as float32. Ignoring HNSW overhead, how many GB do the raw vectors need after scalar (int8) quantization?","answer":15.36,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"GB","hint":"float32 uses 4 bytes per dimension, int8 uses 1.","explain":"10,000,000 x 1,536 = 15.36 billion values. At float32 that is 61.44 GB and at int8, 4 times smaller, it is 15.36 GB. In Azure AI Search the vector index quota is a memory quota and HNSW adds 1 to 20 percent on top. Binary quantization goes further, up to about 28 times smaller and best above 1,024 dimensions, and rescoring with oversampling recovers recall.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q6","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"For an exact question the right document sits at rank 120 of the first-stage results. Enabling the semantic ranker does not surface it. Why?","options":["The semantic ranker summarizes at most 2,048 tokens per document, so long documents always score low","It reranks only the top 50 results of BM25 or RRF and cannot rerun the query to find new documents","Semantic ranking needs an `orderBy` clause to place the best document first in the result list","Semantic ranking works only on `search=*`, which skips first-stage scoring for the whole query"],"answer":1,"explain":"A reranker reorders a candidate list and cannot add to it, so a document outside the top 50 never reaches it. Fix recall in the first stage: hybrid retrieval, better chunks, a larger candidate set (k plus maxTextRecallSize at least 50) and metadata filters. An `orderBy` clause returns HTTP 400 with semantic ranking, an empty or `*` query cannot be semantically ranked, and the 2,048-token cap limits what is read per document, not which documents are considered.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r8"},{"id":"w19-l7-q7","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"An assistant streams answers, and compliance says no unfiltered text may ever reach the screen. Which content filter streaming mode do you choose?","options":["Asynchronous filter mode, because tokens stream at once and the filter signal always arrives before the text is shown","Asynchronous filter mode, because its filtering signal is guaranteed over the whole length of every answer","Filtering off while streaming, with a nightly review of saved transcripts to catch any problems later","Default streaming, which buffers text, filters it and returns it in chunks at the cost of some delay"],"answer":3,"explain":"In default streaming the text is buffered, filtered and then returned in chunks, so nothing unfiltered is shown, at a latency cost. In asynchronous filter mode the signal is guaranteed only within a roughly 1,000-character window, so text can be shown before it is judged. Flip to asynchronous mode only when perceived latency matters more and a late retraction is acceptable, for example low-risk content with post-hoc redaction.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q8","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which statements about native document-level ACL queries in Azure AI Search (preview as of 2026-10-13) are correct? Choose all that apply.","options":["The caller's Entra token goes in the `x-ms-query-source-authorization` header","From 2025-11-01-preview an API key or admin role bypasses the ACL filter","A request with no user token returns only public documents","If group resolution through Microsoft Graph fails, the query returns partial results","Permission fields use Entra object ids, not user principal names"],"answers":[0,2,4],"explain":"The user's token travels in the query-source header, a missing token returns only public documents from 2025-11-01-preview, and permission fields hold object ids. Since that version the ACL filter applies even with an API key or an admin role, so the bypass statement is wrong, and a Graph failure returns a 5xx error rather than a partial result, which is the safe default.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w19-l7-q9","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"An MCP server receives an access token from the client and forwards it unchanged to a downstream API to save a token exchange. What is wrong?","options":["Token passthrough, which the specification forbids: a server must accept only tokens issued for it","Nothing is wrong, as long as the token's scopes already cover the downstream API it is forwarded to","The token must first be wrapped in an `Mcp-Session-Id` header before it can be forwarded downstream","The token is too short-lived, so the server should cache it and reuse it across users and sessions"],"answer":0,"explain":"MCP servers must not accept tokens that were not issued for them, and passing a client's token downstream makes the server a confused deputy. Clients send the RFC 8707 resource parameter so tokens are audience-bound, and the server obtains its own downstream token, for example by an on-behalf-of exchange. Matching scopes do not fix a wrong audience, the session header is unrelated, and sharing a cached token across users is a leak.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r40"},{"id":"w19-l7-q10","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client sends a refinement to a task in the COMPLETED state, using that task's id, and the server rejects it. What is the right continuation?","options":["Task ids are client-generated, so the id collided: generate a new UUID and resend to the same task","The server lacks the push notification capability: enable it on the Agent Card and resend the message","A terminal task cannot restart, so send the refinement as a new task in the same contextId","COMPLETED is an interrupted state: call SubscribeToTask first to resume the task before sending"],"answer":2,"explain":"COMPLETED, FAILED, CANCELED and REJECTED are terminal, so refinements start a new task in the same contextId and may point back with referenceTaskIds. INPUT_REQUIRED and AUTH_REQUIRED are the interrupted states, where a message continues the same task. Task ids are server-generated, and push notifications concern delivery of updates, not whether a task can restart.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r42"},{"id":"w19-l7-q11","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change in the workload most justifies moving from one agent to parallel subagents?","options":["The subtasks begin writing to the same files and must agree on a number of shared decisions","The work becomes read-heavy research over independent sources, worth several times the tokens","The prompt grows by ten sentences of style rules that the model sometimes follows and sometimes ignores","The latency target tightens, but each step needs the previous step's output"],"answer":1,"explain":"Independent reads parallelize, and Anthropic reports parallel subagents cutting research time by up to 90 percent while multi-agent systems use about 15 times the tokens of chat, so the value per task must justify the cost. Parallel writers that share decisions conflict, which is Cognition's argument against them. Style rules are a prompt fix, and sequential dependencies cannot run in parallel. Start at the lowest level of complexity that meets the need.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r45"},{"id":"w19-l7-q12","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After a deployment, resuming from last week's workflow checkpoint fails. The new build renamed several executors and rewired two edges. What explains it?","options":["Checkpoints store only the final output, so an intermediate step can never be resumed from them","Checkpoints are written once before the first superstep, so later steps are always recomputed from scratch","File checkpoint storage cannot be read by a newer build of the same package, whatever else changed","A checkpoint is tied to the workflow's topology and executor ids, so renaming or rewiring breaks resume"],"answer":3,"explain":"A checkpoint is created at the end of each superstep and holds executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses and shared state, so it can only be restored into a workflow with the same topology and executor identities (in .NET, agents also need a stable Id and an unchanged Name). Version workflows deliberately and drain or migrate old runs. The first two options misstate what is stored and when, and storage format is not the issue.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r50"},{"id":"w19-l7-q13","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"You want to canary a new hosted agent version on 5 percent of traffic. What works?","options":["Deploy the version as a second agent and split traffic in the client or a gateway","Set a 5 percent traffic weight between two versions on the same agent endpoint, since it routes by weight","Add a second sandbox size to the same version so that a share of sessions receives the new build","Create two sessions per user and let the platform alternate between versions as requests arrive"],"answer":0,"explain":"A hosted agent endpoint serves one version with 100 percent of traffic and traffic splitting is not supported, so a canary is a second agent with the split done by the caller or a gateway. Compare evaluation and trace metrics, then promote. Sandbox sizes belong to a version, and sessions do not alternate between versions.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r53"},{"id":"w19-l7-q14","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A voice agent keeps cutting in when a user pauses mid-sentence. It uses `server_vad`. What do you try first?","options":["Raise the output sampling rate so replies start faster than the pause the user takes","Switch to a larger model, because turn endings depend on how deeply the model reasons","Use `semantic_vad` with low eagerness, so end of turn depends on meaning, not only silence","Turn detection off, and commit the audio buffer manually every 100 ms of incoming audio"],"answer":2,"explain":"`server_vad` is silence-based, so a thinking pause looks like the end of a turn. `semantic_vad` with eagerness low waits longer for a finished thought, and in noisy settings the silence threshold and duration can also be tuned. Sampling rate and model size do not change turn detection, and committing the buffer manually is the pattern for transcription-only models, not a fix for interruptions.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q15","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Four queries have their first relevant result at ranks 1, 3, none and 2. What is the mean reciprocal rank (MRR)?","answer":0.458,"tolerancePct":1,"hint":"A query with no relevant result contributes 0.","explain":"The reciprocal ranks are 1, 0.333, 0 and 0.5, which sum to 1.833, and dividing by 4 gives 0.458. MRR rewards getting one relevant result high and ignores everything after it, so pair it with recall at k when the answer needs several chunks.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w19-l7-q16","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Spans from an Agent Framework agent and from the MCP server it calls show up as separate traces. The tool is a provider-managed MCP tool. Why?","options":["MCP forbids trace context in messages, so joining the two traces is impossible by design of the protocol","It injects `traceparent` into `params._meta` only for MCP sessions it opens, not provider-managed tools","A 5 percent sampling ratio always drops the child spans of tool calls, so they never join the parent","The server needs the trace id in the `Mcp-Session-Id` header before it can join the existing trace"],"answer":1,"explain":"MCP carries W3C trace context as `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` in `params._meta`, and the 2026-07-28 revision reserves those keys. Agent Framework Python injects them only for sessions it opens (MCPStreamableHTTPTool, MCPStdioTool, MCPWebsocketTool), so a provider-managed tool never receives the context. Use a framework-opened MCP tool or propagate at a gateway. Sampling drops whole traces, not individual children, and the session header is unrelated.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w19-l7-q17","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A deployment has 90,000 TPM. Each request has 700 prompt tokens and sets max_tokens to 2,300, and the TPM check counts prompt plus max_tokens up front. At most how many requests per minute pass the check?","answer":30,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","hint":"Divide the quota by tokens counted per request.","explain":"Each request counts 700 + 2,300 = 3,000 tokens against the limit, so 90,000 / 3,000 = 30 requests per minute. Setting max_tokens near the real answer length, say 500, counts 1,200 per request and admits 75. This is why a 429 can appear below the quota you think you are using.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r31"},{"id":"w19-l7-q18","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each date to what the EU AI Act timeline puts there.","pairs":[{"l":"2025-02-02","r":"Prohibitions and AI literacy apply"},{"l":"2025-08-02","r":"General-purpose AI obligations apply"},{"l":"2026-08-02","r":"Article 50 transparency rules apply and enforcement starts"},{"l":"2027-12-02","r":"Annex III high-risk rules apply, as moved by the AI Omnibus"}],"explain":"These dates anchor a compliance conversation. Prohibitions and AI literacy came first, general-purpose AI obligations followed on 2025-08-02, Article 50 transparency applies from 2026-08-02 and the Omnibus moved Annex III high-risk rules to 2027-12-02, with Annex I rules on 2028-08-02. Verify against the current official timeline before relying on a date.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r59"},{"id":"w19-l7-q19","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A reasoning-model call returns status `incomplete` with no visible text, and `max_output_tokens` is 800. What is the cause and the fix?","options":["The model refused silently, so add a refusal handler to the client and surface the refusal text","The content filter blocked the answer, so lower the severity threshold for that content category","Streaming was off, so visible text was dropped by the client; turn streaming on to receive it","Reasoning tokens used the output budget before any visible text, so reserve 25,000 tokens at first"],"answer":3,"explain":"Reasoning tokens bill as output and share the context window, so a small `max_output_tokens` can be spent before any visible text, which returns `incomplete`. OpenAI and Azure advise reserving at least 25,000 tokens when starting out, then tuning reasoning effort. Refusals and content filters produce different statuses, and streaming changes delivery, not the budget.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r27"},{"id":"w19-l7-q20","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"An agent reads customer emails (untrusted input), searches internal HR files (sensitive access) and can send external email. Which design follows the Agents Rule of Two?","options":["Keep all three properties and add a stronger system prompt that forbids leaking HR data","Keep all three properties and log every external send for audit after the fact","Remove one of the three, or require human approval before the external send happens","Keep all three properties and run the model at temperature 0 so behavior is deterministic"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Rule of Two says a session should hold no more than two of untrusted input, sensitive access and the ability to change state or communicate externally, and should require human approval or an equivalent when all three are needed. Prompts are not controls, audit after the fact does not prevent exfiltration and temperature 0 does not make injected instructions safe.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q21","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A static 60,000-token policy handbook is queried 40 times a minute by one assistant. Which design is the most defensible first choice?","options":["Fine-tune a model on the handbook, because the content is static and rarely changes","Cache the handbook as a stable prefix and compare it with RAG on a golden set first","Always build a vector index first, because long prompts are never accurate enough for policy text","Send the handbook uncached on every call, because the cache write premium costs more than it saves"],"answer":1,"explain":"A corpus under roughly 200,000 tokens can go in the prompt, and with 40 queries a minute the prefix is reused many times inside the TTL, so one write at 1.25 times and cheap reads at 0.1 times beat resending it. Long-context accuracy can still fall well before the advertised window (in NoLiMa, 11 of 13 models that claim 128K or more dropped below 50 percent of their short-context baseline at 32K [[r87]]), so measure against RAG. Flip to RAG when the corpus outgrows the window, changes often enough to bust the cache, needs per-user access control or needs passage-level citations.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r4"},{"id":"w19-l7-q22","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order a secure RAG request from first step to last.","items":["Authenticate the user and resolve their groups","Screen the input, then retrieve with the permission filter applied","Rerank the filtered candidates","Generate the answer with citations from the allowed chunks","Check groundedness and constrain the rendered output","Emit trace spans and a retrieval log without raw content"],"explain":"Identity comes first because the filter needs it. Retrieval must apply the permission filter before any reranking or generation, so the model never sees forbidden chunks. Output checks run on the finished answer, and telemetry closes the request with references rather than raw text.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q23","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which practices make an LLM-as-judge setup more reliable? Choose all that apply.","options":["Run pairwise comparisons in both orders and count only consistent verdicts","Give the judge a reference answer for math or factual grading","Use the same model that wrote the answer as the judge, to keep style consistent","Calibrate the judge against a human-labelled sample and track agreement","Prefer the longest answer when scores tie, because it is usually more complete"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Swapping order cancels position bias, a reference answer cut math grading failures from 14 of 20 to 3 of 20 in MT-Bench [[r73]], and a human-labelled calibration set shows whether the judge can be trusted at all. A judge that grades its own model's output tends to favor it, and preferring length rewards verbosity rather than correctness.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w19-l7-q24","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each MCP primitive or hint to who controls it or how it is treated.","pairs":[{"l":"Tools","r":"Model-controlled"},{"l":"Resources","r":"Application-controlled"},{"l":"Prompts","r":"User-controlled"},{"l":"Tool annotations","r":"Untrusted unless they come from a trusted server"}],"explain":"Tools are invoked by the model, resources are attached by the application and prompts are chosen by the user. Annotations such as readOnlyHint are hints, and clients must consider them untrusted unless the server is trusted, so never base a security decision on them alone.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r38"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r4","r8","r14","r19","r25","r27","r31","r38","r40","r42","r45","r50","r53","r59","r63","r72","r73","r76","r77","r87"],"takeaways":["Ask the ten questions in order, because each one narrows which options are valid","Name the dial before you name the product, every time","Know the mechanisms behind the numbers: cache pricing, quotas, patch tokens, checkpoints and ACL behavior","End every answer with what would flip the choice and the signal that tells you it did","Date-stamp previews and retirements, because the freshness of a claim is part of the claim"]}],"topics":[]});
